For all of these loss-functions, the set of feasible solutions is convex (with or without box-constraints). Consequently, the hardness of \hfr depends on the loss-function. 
For step-functions, \hfr becomes NP-hard, as they introduce an integrality aspect. For our hardness proof, we use the $||\cdot||_0$-norm as a loss function. The $||\cdot||_0$ norm in $\mathbb{R}^n$ is defined as the number of non-zero entries, i.e. 
$ ||x||_0 = \sum_{i=0}^n \iota_i, \quad  \iota_i = \begin{cases} 0 & \text{if } x_i = 0,\\ 1 & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$

\begin{theorem}[NP-Hardness for $\ell_0$]
Minimizing the loss of the $\|\cdot\|_0$ norm for \hfr is NP-hard, where for $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n$:
$ \|\mathbf{x}\|_0 = |\{i : x_i \neq 0\}| $
\end{theorem}

