\begin{abstract}
Knowledge-based Vision Question Answering (KB-VQA) extends general Vision Question Answering (VQA) by not only requiring the understanding of visual and textual inputs but also extensive range of knowledge, enabling significant advancements across various real-world applications. KB-VQA introduces unique challenges, including the alignment of heterogeneous information from diverse modalities and sources, the retrieval of relevant knowledge from noisy or large-scale repositories, and the execution of complex reasoning to infer answers from the combined context. With the advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs), KB-VQA systems have also undergone a notable transformation, where LLMs serve as powerful knowledge repositories, retrieval-augmented generators and strong reasoners. Despite substantial progress, no comprehensive survey currently exists that systematically organizes and reviews the existing KB-VQA methods. This survey aims to fill this gap by establishing a structured taxonomy of KB-VQA approaches, and categorizing the systems into main stages: knowledge representation, knowledge retrieval, and knowledge reasoning. By exploring various knowledge integration techniques and identifying persistent challenges, this work also outlines promising future research directions, providing a foundation for advancing KB-VQA models and their applications.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Multimodal systems, Knowledge modeling, Knowledge management 
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

\IEEEPARstart{V}{ision} question answering (VQA)~\cite{Agrawal2017VQA:Answering} has long stood as one of the most compelling challenges residing at the intersection of computer vision (CV) and natural language processing (NLP). While conventional VQA systems aim to answer questions directly from visual context, Knowledge-based VQA (KB-VQA)~\cite{Marino2019OK-VQA:Knowledge} presents a particular extension, where it necessitates not only understanding the visual and textual inputs but also requiring the effective incorporation and reasoning over knowledge repositories. As demonstrated in Fig.\textcolor{red}{~\ref{examples}}, conventional VQA involves questions that can be directly answered by processing and describing an image, exemplified by questions like \texttt{"How many kids are in the bed?"}. In contrast, KB-VQA often requires domain-specific understanding. For instance, answering the question \texttt{"What days might I most commonly go to this building?"} involves commonsense knowledge that \texttt{people often go to church for praying on Sundays}. Similarly, answering \texttt{"What is the length of this bird?"} is not possible without incorporating expert knowledge about bird biology. Through incorporating commonsense knowledge, open-world knowledge and domain-specific knowledge, the KB-VQA systems have its important impact in making advancements across real-world scenarios, including medical diagnosis~\cite{Xia2024MMed-RAG:Models, Naeem2024Path-RAG:Answering} and interactive education area~\cite{Kembhavi2017AreComprehension}.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
   \centerline{\includegraphics{kb-vqa_examples.pdf}}
    \caption{A comparison between conventional VQA question and KB-VQA questions with different knowledge requirements.}
    \label{examples}
\end{figure}
The integration of knowledge in KB-VQA brings unique challenges that are absent in conventional VQA. First, multi-modal \textbf{knowledge fusion} remains a fundamental challenge. Unlike traditional VQA, where the model operates solely on image and text pairs, KB-VQA systems must effectively align retrieved knowledge with both the visual and textual modalities in a fine-grained level. This requires sophisticated cross-modal representation learning mechanisms to bridge the gap between knowledge representations and high-dimensional image-question features. Second, as the system must determine the relevant pieces of knowledge from vast and heterogeneous sources, including structured knowledge graphs, unstructured text corpora, or pre-trained language models, identifying the most pertinent facts by effective \textbf{knowledge retrieval} techniques is another critical task,while filtering out noise is non-trivial, particularly when dealing with ambiguous or context-dependent queries. \textbf{Knowledge reasoning} further presents another major challenge in deriving accurate responses. Even when relevant knowledge is retrieved, simply having access to it does not guarantee that the model can effectively utilize it. Generally, KB-VQA requires strong reasoning abilities to deeply understand multiple pieces of information and infer implicit relational connections that are not explicitly stated in the input. For even more challenging scenarios, relevant information could be hidden in extremely long chunks of knowledge; to answer logically-complex questions, multi-step inference might also be required.

Existing survey papers on general VQA~\cite{Zakari2022VQAChallenges, Srivastava2021VisualAnalysis, Kuang2025NaturalSurvey} and multimodal learning techniques~\cite{Jabeen2023ALearning, Wu2023MultimodalSurvey} primarily focus on the integration of multimodal data, treating KB-VQA as one of the many applications. As discussed above, KB-VQA possesses its unique challenges that can not be appropriately addressed by method frameworks developed for general VQA tasks. The rise of Large Language Models (LLMs) has further reshaped the field, which not only act as knowledge sources with its large-scale implicit knowledge but also enable complex reasoning by well-designed prompts, such as chain-of-thought or dynamic agent-based retrieval, offering new solutions to the long-standing KB-VQA challenges. 

As the field rapidly advances, a comprehensive overview of the current landscape of KB-VQA studies is urgently needed--one that focuses specifically on knowledge fusion, knowledge retrieval, and knowledge reasoning techniques, while also capturing emerging trends in the LLM-driven evolution of KB-VQA. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first comprehensive survey to provide a holistic overview of this evolving field. It aims to systematically review notable KB-VQA approaches, with the following key contributions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose a structured taxonomy framework for categorizing existing KB-VQA methods to highlight their distinctions and commonalities.
    \item We analyze the architectural designs and knowledge incorporation mechanisms at each stage of systems to fully understand the life cycle of knowledge in KB-VQA.
    \item We identify key challenges and limitations in current approaches and offering an outlook for future advancements.
\end{itemize}

By offering a structured analysis, we seek to provide valuable insights into the development of KB-VQA and highlight promising directions for future research. The covered works in this survey are collected from top-tier conferences and journals, ensuring a comprehensive and up-to-date review of the field. The existing research is structured as follows. Sec.\ref{def} defines the KB-VQA task and its setting. Sec.~\ref{know_sources} examines various knowledge sources and their influence on system paradigms. Sec.~\ref{representation}-\ref{knowledge retreival section} provide a comprehensive overview of the main components of KB-VQA, including knowledge representation, retrieval, and reasoning. Sec.~\ref{dataset_summary} reviews popular benchmarks and discusses potential future research directions.

