\subsection{Explicit Knowledge}
KB-VQA systems leverage explicit open-domain knowledge from either structured sources like ConceptNet~\cite{Speer2016ConceptNetKnowledge} and WikiData~\cite{Vrandecic2014Wikidata}, or unstructured resources such as GoogleSearch~\cite{Luo2021Weakly-SupervisedAnswering} and Wikipedia~\cite{Lin2023Fine-grainedAnswering}. These systems typically adopt a multi-step pipeline of knowledge retrieval followed by retrieving answer for generation—referred as \textcolor{black}{\textsc{Retrieval-based Systems}}. The choice between structured and unstructured knowledge significantly impacts both retrieval strategies and reasoning capabilities.

Structured knowledge graphs offer a well-organized representation of facts, typically formatted as triples \texttt{(head entity, relation, tail entity)}~\cite{Ji2022AApplications}. In KB-VQA, images and questions are often transformed into structured queries that match predefined knowledge graphs. For instance, Wu et al.\cite{Wu2018ImageKnowledge} introduced a cascaded CNN-LSTM framework that generates image attribute tags, which are then converted into SQL-like queries to extract relevant facts from DBpedia\cite{Auer2007DBpedia:Data}. However, such approaches heavily depend on predefined knowledge facts, restricting their adaptability to novel questions. To address this limitation, KG-Aug~\cite{Li2020BoostingAggregation} proposes a context-aware knowledge aggregation framework, which retrieves large-scale knowledge graph information without requiring ground-truth knowledge supervision. Instead of relying on strict query-matching, KG-Aug models contextual entities and candidate answer entities, incorporating external knowledge to enhance feature representations.

Systems relying on unstructured knowledge sources often emphasize on free-form retrieval and reasoning process. TRiG~\cite{Gao2022Transform-Retrieve-Generate:Answering} extracts useful knowledge from encyclopedic passages via Dense Passage Retrieval (DPR)~\cite{Karpukhin2020DenseAnswering}. It emphasizes on understanding images within language spaces with three-level image-language conversions, including image captioning~\cite{Li2020Oscar:Tasks}, dense labeling~\cite{Anderson2018Bottom-UpAnswering} and OCR~\cite{Jain2021SelectAnswering}. Following the idea of verbalizing image contexts, RA-VQA~\cite{Lin2022RetrievalKnowledge} presents a strong joint end-to-end training framework which includes a knowledge retriever and an answer generator for acquiring more relevant knowledge from Google Search~\cite{Luo2021Weakly-SupervisedAnswering}. While unstructured knowledge sources provide richer contextual understanding and broader knowledge coverage, they often introduce noisy or irrelevant information that can mislead the reasoning process. Effectively mitigating this noise remains an open challenge in KB-VQA research.

\subsection{Implicit Knowledge}
The scope of knowledge sources has recently expanded with the advancement of large pre-trained models. Through extensive pretraining on massive datasets, LLMs and multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have acquired vast amounts of knowledge, emerging as powerful implicit knowledge repositories. This development has led to the rise of new paradigm in KB-VQA, such as \textcolor{black}{\textsc{prompt-based method}}, where pre-trained models serve as knowledge banks, retrieving implicit information through carefully crafted prompts.

By constructing prompts that integrate context information, LLMs such as GPT-3~\cite{Brown2020LanguageLearners}, LLaMA~\cite{Touvron2023LLaMA:Models}, and T5-XL~\cite{Raffel2019ExploringTransformer} have proven effective in KB-VQA, particularly for questions requiring extensive common-sense or general world knowledge. PICa~\cite{Yang2022AnVQA} is the first work that leverages frozen GPT-3~\cite{Brown2020LanguageLearners} for multimodal tasks. It feeds a few in-context examples alongside the target question to the large language model, enabling which to learn and apply patterns for answering image-based questions through demonstration. PnP-VQA~\cite{Tiong2022Plug-and-PlayTraining} is another impressive work that proposes a modular framework without training that converts the image into question-relevant captions to activate the capacity of UnifiedQAv2~\cite{Khashabi2020UNIFIEDQA:System}. However, PromptCap~\cite{Hu2023PromptCap:Captioning} argue that converting the image into generic texts can exclude essential visual information. Therefore, they introduce a question-guided image caption model to serve as a bridge between images and language models. %Being extended from PnP-VQA~\cite{Tiong2022Plug-and-PlayTraining} to further harness the advantages of in-context learning. %Prophet~\cite{Yu2023Prophet:Answering} further challenge that prior works often verbalize visual content and the text-based input cannot fully depict the required visual information, therefore limiting LLMs' ability to answer the question. To address this, Prophet~\cite{Yu2023Prophet:Answering} first trains a vanilla VQA model to generate "answer heuristics" before prompting the LLM.


Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) such as InstructBLIP~\cite{Dai2023InstructBLIP:Tuning}, BLIP2~\cite{Li2023BLIP-2:Models}, and PaLM-E~\cite{Driess2023PaLM-E:Model} have shown strong capabilities in aligning visual and textual modalities. On the OK-VQA~\cite{Marino2019OK-VQA:Knowledge} benchmark, InstructBLIP achieves 57.62\% zero-shot accuracy, while PaLM-E sets a new state-of-the-art with 66.46\%, highlighting their effectiveness in capturing multimodal knowledge. Building on this, GeReA~\cite{Ma2024GeReA:Answering} leverages MLLMs as implicit knowledge sources by prompting them with in-context examples, followed by answer generation using a reasoning model (FiD~\cite{Izacard2021LeveragingAnswering}).
Compared to explicit knowledge sources, implicit knowledge in pre-trained models enables flexible, zero-shot reasoning, allowing KB-VQA systems to answer questions without explicit retrieval. However, since this knowledge is embedded within model parameters and cannot be easily updated, it lacks interpretability and may contain factual errors in responses~\cite{Mallen2023WhenMemories, Min2023FActScore:Generation}, which are still challenging in KB-VQA.



\subsection{Hybrid Knowledge}
To harness the benefit of implicit and explicit knowledge at the same time, works utilize hybrid knowledge sources are proposed. Earlier works such as KAT~\cite{Gui2021KAT:Vision-and-Language} uses a contrastive-learning-based module to retrieve knowledge entries from an explicit knowledge base, and uses GPT-3~\cite{Brown2020LanguageLearners} to retrieve implicit knowledge with supporting evidence. Then the knowledge will be integrated jointly by a reasoning module and the decoder. REVIVE~\cite{Lin2022REVIVE:Answering} further highlights the importance of regional information and proposes a novel Regional Feature Extraction Module with GLIP~\cite{Li2022GroundedPre-training} as the backbone. With the exploit of regional information, question and context to retrieve different types of knowledge. 

% RAG paradigm % The difference between classic hybrid methods and RAG-based methods.
Similarly, RA-VQAv2~\cite{Lin2023Fine-grainedAnswering} focuses on fine-grained information processing to improve KB-VQA systems. However, unlike previous approaches, RA-VQAv2 expands the role of LLMs beyond implicit knowledge retrieval; it also exploits them to comprehend question context and perform reasoning. Such type of approach that retrieves external knowledge to augment the generation process of LLMs is also know as \textcolor{black}{\textsc{Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) Methods}}. More recently, the exploit of LLMs have vastly explored from being a knowledge bank. For example, AVIS~\cite{Hu2023AVIS:Agent} proposes an LLM-powered planner to dynamically determine which tool to use at each step and what query to send to it. In particular, the planner can adaptively choose to employ LLM for direct answering or search over external knowledge for relevant answers. Additionally,\cite{Wang2023TowardsAnswering} introduces a multi-agent framework that enhances VLMs through collaborative reasoning between three specialized agents: a VLM-based Responder, an LLM-powered Seeker, and an Integrator, collectively enabling more comprehensive visual understanding by leveraging both vision and language capabilities. Such way of combining LLMs and external knowledge bases further opens up the potential for more adaptive KB-VQA systems. The comparison of each knowledge sources and how they have potential impact on the system are summarized in Table\textcolor{red}{~\ref{knowledge_comparison}}.

