In contrast, \emph{SRTT} and \emph{Greedy} generally produce routes with higher MAIs.
Table~\ref{tab:avgMAI} summarizes the average normalized MAIs of routes generated by different approaches across various scenario setups.

Although \emph{LHK} has the least average normalized MAI, there are scenarios where \emph{Enforced} generates routes with lower MAIs.
For example, in $47$ out of $100$ \emph{8-node} scenarios with a \emph{Grid} distribution, \emph{Enforced} generates routes with MAIs no larger than those of \emph{LHK}.
With a larger number of nodes in the \emph{20-node} scenarios, the likelihood of \emph{Enforced} outperforming \emph{LHK} decreases.
However, in $7$ out of $100$ scenarios with a \emph{Grid} distribution, \emph{Enforced} still performs at least as well as \emph{LKH}.
Nonetheless, \emph{Enforced} achieves the optimal MAI in at least $37\%$ of the \emph{8-node} scenarios, regardless of node distribution.
These empirical results support our claim that minimizing round trip time does not necessarily lead to a smaller MAI, highlighting \emph{Enforced} as a competitive approach for optimizing MAI in patrolling routes.

