Mossel~\cite{Mossel:19deep} provided a rigorous attempt to establish computational hardness, though subsequent work \cite{KoehlerMossel:22,HuangMossel:24} only demonstrated low-degree hardness.
In terms of compositions of functions, 
Telgarsky~\cite{Telgarsky:16} presents a notable result demonstrating an iteratively composed simple function that can be precisely expressed as a deep narrow network, yet requires super-polynomial computational complexity for small network depth.

The second research line establishes learning lower bounds in terms of the Fourier expansion of functions or classes of functions. 
This includes in particular SQ lower bounds in terms of the noise-sensitivity in both the Boolean case~\cite{dachman2014approximate} and the Gaussian case~\cite{diakonikolas2021optimality} as well as sample complexity lower bounds for learning such functions using fully connected neural networks and 
stochastic gradient descent~\cite{ABCKLRZ:22}.
