\section{Method}\label{sec:method}


\subsection{LM-Aligned Semantic Distillation}
\paragraph{Motivation}
Prior works such as SpeechTokenizer~\cite{zhang2024speechtokenizer} and Mimi~\cite{moshi} learn discrete semantic representations by distilling continuous features from self-supervised learning (SSL) models, such as HuBERT~\cite{hubert} or WavLM~\cite{wavlm}, into a semantic quantizer. 
Specifically, a pretrained SSL model serves as a teacher, and its frame-level hidden representations are used as supervision targets for training a student encoder, as depicted in \figref{fig:schematic_a}.
% The training objective is typically formulated as minimizing the mean squared error (MSE) or maximizing cosine similarity between the student output and the corresponding teacher features at each frame. 
While effective in guiding the model toward semantically rich tokens, this approach has key limitations:
First, the SSL teacher’s representation may not align well with LM semantics, as it is trained to capture phonetic regularities rather than semantic abstraction~\cite{choi2024self,wells2022phonetic}.
Second, due to the direct supervision at the feature level, it assumes frame-wise alignment between the teacher and student, which becomes problematic under frame rate reduction.
Mimi~\cite{moshi} attempts to address this by distilling 12.5Hz representations from a 50Hz WavLM teacher, using average pooling to align the temporal resolution.
However, this temporal smoothing remains a rigid approximation and can distort or dilute semantic specificity required for effective alignment with language models.
Moreover, this inherent mismatch may hinder the model’s ability to balance semantic learning with accurate signal reconstruction.
% Lastly, we found that feature-level semantic distillation may negatively impact reconstruction fidelity. One possible explanation is that directly supervising high-level semantic features encourages abstraction that is not well aligned with preserving low-level acoustic detail. 
% This inherent mismatch may hinder the model’s ability to balance semantic learning with accurate signal reconstruction, as suggested by our observation that omitting semantic distillation improves fidelity in ablation study.
% \vspace{-0.2cm}
\paragraph{Reconstruction-driven Distillation from ASR Teacher}
To better align semantic supervision with LM-level representations, we propose to use a pretrained automatic speech recognition (ASR) encoder (Whisper~\cite{whisper}) as the semantic teacher. 
Although Whisper is trained for speech recognition, its encoder outputs are optimized for text prediction and implicitly capture high-level linguistic structures~\cite{choi2024self, whisper, xu2024comparing}. 
% We therefore refer to our approach as \textit{LM-aligned semantic distillation}, as Whisper representations serve as an effective proxy for language model semantics in practice.
% To address these limitations
To overcome the challenge of temporal misalignment under low frame rates, we propose a novel semantic distillation strategy that bypasses direct feature-level supervision and instead employs a reconstruction-driven alignment across different frame rates. 
Specifically, as depicted in \figref{fig:schematic_b}, we reconstruct speech solely from semantic tokens and supervise the tokenizer by minimizing the distance between ASR encoder features of the original and reconstructed waveforms. 
Formally, our semantic distillation loss is defined as:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:distill}
\begin{split}
    \mathcal{L}_\text{distill} &= \left\| f_T(x) - f_T(\hat{x}_\text{sem}) \right\|^2,
\end{split}
\end{align}
where $f_T$ denotes the frozen encoder of a pretrained ASR model (e.g., Whisper), which maps waveforms $x$ to semantic representations. 
Using an auxiliary decoder $\mathcal{G}_\text{aux}$, the reconstructed waveform $\hat{x}_\text{sem} = \mathcal{G}_\text{aux}(z_\text{sem})$ is generated from semantic tokens $z_\text{sem} = \mathcal{Q}_\text{sem}(h_\text{sem}(x))$, where $\mathcal{Q}_\text{sem}$ is the semantic vector quantization module applied to the semantic encoder output $h_\text{sem}(x)$.
% \vspace{-0.2cm}
\paragraph{Decoupling Semantic Distillation from Decoding} 
We emphasize that an auxiliary decoder $\mathcal{G}_\text{aux}$ is used for the reconstruction, without relying on acoustic tokens or any additional supervision. 
Namely, we do not share this decoder with the main reconstruction module. 
This design choice is based on the following considerations:
First, using a shared decoder for both audio reconstruction and semantic distillation may lead to interference between tasks, as the decoder is jointly optimized for potentially conflicting objectives. 
Second, one might attempt to mitigate this interference by freezing the decoder during distillation, allowing it to focus solely on reconstruction. While this can improve fidelity by preventing gradient conflict, we find that it also weakens the effect of distillation, as the decoder no longer adapts to match semantic supervision, which is confirmed through empirical analysis (see \subsecref{sec:ablation}).
% We empirically show such interference in \subsecref{sec:ablation}, where performance degrades when both objectives are handled by a single decoder.
Moreover, we deliberately design this decoder to have limited capacity, functioning as an information bottleneck.
This constraint forces the semantic encoder to produce more expressive and compact representations. 
In \subsecref{sec:ablation}, we demonstrate that using a lightweight decoder indeed leads to stronger semantic representations under LM-alignment evaluation.

% We intentionally introduce an information bottleneck by limiting reconstruction to the semantic quantizer output and using a lightweight decoder.
% Combined with supervision from a frozen ASR encoder, which is likely biased toward semantic content, this design encourages the reconstructed waveform to encode semantic rather than acoustic detail.
% \vspace{-0.2cm}
\paragraph{Theoretical Interpretation}
To better understand the advantage of our reconstruction-driven semantic distillation over conventional feature-level distillation, we provide a theoretical comparison under a simplified probabilistic assumption. 
Let us assume that both feature-level and reconstruction-driven distillation can be framed as minimizing a KL divergence between multivariate Gaussian distributions, where the encoder outputs $f_T(x)$ are treated as a multivariate random variable~\footnote{Although existing feature-level distillation methods typically employ cosine similarity loss, we consider an MSE-based formulation for analytical simplicity.}. 
Namely, 
\begin{align}
\label{eq:feat_recon_distill}
\begin{split}
    \mathcal{L}_\text{distill}^\text{feat} &= MSE(f_S(x), f_T(x)) \propto D_{KL}(P_{f_T}(x)||P_{f_S}(x)), \\
    \mathcal{L}_\text{distill}^\text{recon} &= MSE(f_T(\hat{x}), f_T(x)) \propto D_{KL}(P_{f_T}(x)||P_{f_T}(\hat{x})),
\end{split}
\end{align}
where $f_S$ stands for the student (i.e., the semantic quantizer) trained to approximate $f_T$ through distillation.
Here, we model the distributions as $P_{f_T}(x)=\mathcal{N}(\mu_{f_T}, \sigma_{f_T}I)$ and $P_{f_S}(x)=\mathcal{N}(\mu_{f_S}, \sigma_{f_S}I)$. Under the distributions, the KL divergence for feature-level distillation becomes 
\begin{align}
\label{eq:feat_KL}
\begin{split}
    D_{KL}(P_{f_T}(x)||P_{f_S}(x)) = \frac{1}{2}\bigg(\frac{1}{\sigma_{f_S}}||\mu_{f_S} - \mu_{f_T}||^2 - d + log\text{det}(\sigma_{f_S}\sigma_{f_T}^{-1}I)  + tr(\sigma_{f_S}^{-1}\sigma_{f_T}I)\bigg),
\end{split}
\end{align}
where $tr(\cdot)$ is the trace operator.
We assume $P_{f_T}(\hat{x})=\mathcal{N}(\hat{\mu}_{f_T}, \sigma_{f_T}I)$, based on the empirical observation that ASR encoders are insensitive to small waveform perturbations.
% It is commonly accepted in practice due to the low sensitivity of ASR encoders to waveform-level noise.
Then, the KL divergence for reconstruction-driven distillation becomes
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    D_{KL}(P_{f_T}(x)||P_{f_T}(\hat{x})) = \frac{1}{2}\frac{1}{\sigma_{f_T}}||\hat{\mu}_{f_T} - \mu_{f_T}||^2.
\end{split}
\end{align}

If we consider an idealized setting in which the training data and capacity are sufficient to reach near-optimal solutions for both distillation methods, the following approximations are expected to hold at convergence: $\mu_{f_S} = \mathbb{E}_x[f_S(x)] \approx \mathbb{E}_x[f_T(x)]$ and $\hat{\mu}_{f_T} = \mathbb{E}_x[f_T(\hat{x})] \approx \mathbb{E}_x[f_T(x)]$. 
Under these assumptions, the following inequality holds when $\sigma_{f_S} < \sigma_{f_T}$:
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    D_{KL}(P_{f_T}(x)||P_{f_S}(x)) > D_{KL}(P_{f_T}(x)||P_{f_T}(\hat{x})).
\end{split}
\end{align}

It is noted that, from bias-variance trade-off, MSE-trained models tend to underestimate the target variance, that is $\sigma_{f_S} < \sigma_{f_T}$. 
In this case, the term $-d + log\text{det}(\sigma_{f_S}\sigma_{f_T}^{-1}I)  + tr(\sigma_{f_S}^{-1}\sigma_{f_T}I)$ in~\equref{eq:feat_KL} is always larger than zero. 
This suggests that feature-level distillation may incur an irreducible gap in KL divergence due to the variance underestimation. 
In contrast, reconstruction-driven distillation can achieve zero KL divergence under reasonable assumptions, offering a stronger form of semantic alignment when combined with an ASR semantic teacher.
We provide empirical support for this interpretation in \subsecref{sec:ablation} by comparing the two distillation strategies using the same ASR teacher.


\subsection{SpeechTokenizer Model Improvement}
\paragraph{Split RVQ with Dual Encoder}
\citet{moshi} demonstrates that applying semantic distillation to the first level of a single RVQ stack improves phonetic discriminability but degrades reconstruction fidelity.
This degradation is hypothesized to stem from the need for the first RVQ level to balance semantic supervision and residual modeling, thereby compromising signal fidelity.
To mitigate this conflict, Mimi~\cite{moshi} introduces a split structure—distilling semantics into a plain VQ, and applying a 7-level RVQ in parallel for acoustic details.
Motivated by this design, we adopt a similar split quantization architecture in LM-SPT, consisting of a dedicated semantic vector quantizer $\mathcal{Q}_\text{sem}$ and a residual vector quantizer stack $\mathcal{Q}_\text{ac}$ for acoustic encoding.

Moreover, inspired by prior work on disentangled representation learning~\cite{qu2024tokenflow,ye2025codec}, we adopt a dual encoder architecture, where the semantic and acoustic encoders are separately optimized to capture high-level semantic information and low-level acoustic details, respectively.  
In contrast to SpeechTokenizer~\cite{zhang2024speechtokenizer} and Mimi~\cite{moshi}, which either use a shared encoder or partially disentangled semantic and acoustic signals via quantization, our model explicitly separates the encoding process at the architectural level.
Formally, semantic vector quantization is applied to the semantic encoder output, defined as $z_\text{sem} = \mathcal{Q}_\text{sem}(h_\text{sem}(x))$, where $h_\text{sem}(x)$ is the output of the semantic encoder.  
Similarly, acoustic vector quantization is applied to the acoustic encoder output, defined as $z_\text{ac} = \mathcal{Q}_\text{ac}(h_\text{ac}(x))$, where $h_\text{ac}(x)$ is the output of the acoustic encoder.  
We use the same encoder architecture in our dual encoder and follow the encoder architecture as the encoder of Mimi.
In~\subsecref{sec:ablation}, we empirically show that this dual encoder improves both reconstruction fidelity and semantic alignment, particularly when compared to models using a shared encoder.

\paragraph{Improved Decoder}
Transposed convolution based upsampling decoder referred to as \textit{mirrored decoder} is commonly used to reconstruct waveforms from compressed frame representations in neural codecs, but it has been reported to introduce undesired tonal artifacts, degrading reconstruction fidelity~\cite{upsampling_artifacts}.
Vocos~\cite{siuzdak2024vocos} addresses this issue by preserving frame-level temporal resolution and reconstructing waveforms through inverse Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT), following a stack of 1D convolutional blocks.
This approach has also been successfully adopted in recent works such as WavTokenizer~\cite{wavtokenizer}, and we follow the same motivation in applying the Vocos decoder within LM-SPT to improve reconstruction fidelity.

\paragraph{Training Objective}
We build LM-SPT on top of SpeechTokenizer\cite{zhang2024speechtokenizer}, and thus adopt the same set of training losses, including the reconstruction loss, discriminative loss, and feature matching loss.
The only difference is that, due to our use of a split RVQ architecture following Mimi~\cite{moshi}, the commitment loss is applied separately to the semantic and acoustic codebooks.
Further details on the training objectives and hyperparameters are provided in \autoref{app:obj}
