\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Experimental Setups}
\paragraph{Baselines} 
We select the recent codec models with varying frame rates as the baselines for LM-SPT. 
The official weights are employed for EnCodec~\cite{encodec}, SpeechTokenizer~\cite{zhang2024speechtokenizer}, WavTokenizer~\cite{wavtokenizer}, and Mimi~\cite{moshi}.
Additionally, to compare the proposed method across the three different frame rates, we train the feature-level semantic distillation models of SpeechTokenizer and Mimi under the same experimental conditions as LM-SPT.
HuBERT~\cite{hubert} and WavLM~\cite{wavlm} are used as the SSL teachers for SpeechTokenizer and Mimi, respectively, as reported in their original experiments.
As the official training code for Mimi is not publicly available, we implement Mimi using the training framework of SpeechTokenizer~\footnote{\url{https://github.com/ZhangXInFD/SpeechTokenizer}}.
We denote the Mimi baselines we trained as \textit{Mimi (R)} in all results.

\paragraph{Speech tokenizers} 
Following the prior work~\cite{zhang2024speechtokenizer}, we train all baseline speech tokenizers and LM-SPT on the LibriSpeech dataset~\cite{librispeech}, which contains approximately 1,000 hours of English read speech at a 16kHz sampling rate.
For evaluation, we use the \textit{test-clean} split of the LibriSpeech dataset.
We investigate three different frame rates—25Hz, 12.5Hz, and 6.25Hz—chosen to explore the trade-off between compression and reconstruction fidelity.
Each frame rate is paired with a corresponding codebook size: 1024 for 25Hz, 2048 for 12.5Hz, and 4096 for 6.25Hz, respectively.
During training, we randomly crop a 6-second audio segment for the 25Hz and 12.5Hz models, and a 5.6-second segment for the 6.25Hz models, ensuring similar token sequence lengths across different frame rates.
For training, we follow the overall optimization strategy of SpeechTokenizer, with minor modifications. 
Specifically, we train for 20 epochs and use a learning rate of 2e-4. Further training details are provided in \autoref{app:impl}.
Training is conducted on 8 NVIDIA A100 (80GB) GPUs for approximately 40 hours. 

Note that, unlike Mimi~\cite{moshi}, which was trained on a large-scale dataset comprising about 9M-hours of speech,
LM-SPT is trained on the much smaller 1K-hour LibriSpeech dataset.
To investigate the effect of training data scale, we construct a 60K-hour bilingual dataset by combining the LibriHeavy corpus~\cite{libriheavy} with an additional 10K-hours of web-crawled Korean speech.
We train a 12.5Hz LM-SPT model on this large-scale dataset for 3 epochs, and denote this model as \textit{LM-SPT (L)} in the results.
\input{./tabs/main_slm}
\paragraph{Speech language models}
To evaluate how well each speech tokenizer integrates with a LM, we extend the LM vocabulary using the token codebook of each tokenizer and train LMs on two representative downstream tasks: \textit{Text-to-Speech} (TTS) as a generation task, and \textit{Speech-to-Text} (STT) as an understanding task.
For STT, the LM is given only the semantic token sequence from each tokenizer.
% This allows us to evaluate the alignment quality of semantic tokens with text-level representations.
For TTS, we follow the practice of prior token-based codec models and use parallel decoding with one-frame delay~\cite{copet2023simple,moshi}.
For tokenizers with multiple quantizers (i.e., EnCodec, SpeechTokenizer, Mimi, and LM-SPT), we append 7 acoustic token heads alongside a single semantic token head.
For STT, we use a greedy decoding to generate the corresponding text tokens.
For TTS, we apply a greedy decoding for both semantic and acoustic token prediction with repetition penalty of 1.3.
All models are trained on the LibriSpeech dataset~\cite{librispeech} under consistent settings across all frame rates.
For evaluation, we use the \textit{test-clean} split of the LibriSpeech dataset.
Each LM is trained for a total of 10K global steps with an effective batch size of 128 (approximately 4.5 epochs).
We evaluate on two different LM backbones of varying scales: Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct~\cite{qwen25} and LLaMA3.2-3B-Instruct~\cite{llama3}, to assess the scalability and robustness of integration across model sizes.
For optimization, we use the AdamW~\cite{adamw} optimizer with a learning rate of 2e-4, cosine decay scheduler, and weight decay of 0.01.
A SLM training is performed on using the same 8 NVIDIA A100 (80GB) GPUs for around 1.5–3 hours for 0.5B-scale LM and 3–6 hours for 3B-scale LM.
 % Two language models (LMs) were evaluated in the study: Qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct and LLaMA3.2-3b-instruct. Both models utilized parallel decoding with multiple linear heads, employing a one-frame delay pattern in both input and output to ensure effective alignment of temporal sequences.

