\subsection{Main Results}
\paragraph{Reconstruction performance}
The reconstruction results summarized in \tabref{tab:main_recon} clearly show that LM-SPT consistently outperforms baseline models across all evaluated frame rates. 
We also observe that increasing the training data scale leads to improved tokenizer reconstruction fidelity (see LM-SPT vs. LM-SPT (L) at 12.5Hz in \tabref{tab:main_recon}).
% At 25Hz, LM-SPT achieves the lowest WER of 3.19, along with superior perceptual metrics—UTMOS of 3.75, DNSMOS of 3.27, and Similarity score of 0.98. This advantage persists even at reduced frame rates. Specifically, at 12.5Hz, LM-SPT (L) notably reduces WER to 3.33 while maintaining high perceptual quality. Furthermore, under highly compressed conditions at 6.25Hz, LM-SPT continues to demonstrate robustness, achieving a significantly lower WER of 8.35 and better perceptual metrics compared to competing methods. 
% These results underscore the model’s capability in preserving both semantic clarity and acoustic fidelity even under challenging compression scenarios.

\paragraph{Downstream performance}
As shown in \tabref{tab:main_slm}, LM-SPT demonstrates competitive performances on the STT task across all frame rates.
At relatively low compression levels (i.e., higher frame rates such as 25Hz and 50Hz), SpeechTokenizer achieves strong STT performances.
We hypothesize that this is due to the preservation of fine-grained phonetic information distilled from the HuBERT teacher when the frame rate is sufficiently high.
However, as the compression rate increases (i.e., frame rate decreases), its performance degrades significantly.
% —highlighting its limited robustness to high compression.
For the TTS task, LM-SPT consistently outperforms all other tokenizers by a significant margin, demonstrating its effectiveness in semantic representation and generation quality.

In the appendix, we present reconstruction performance and downstream performance on held-out datasets (see \autoref{app:held-out}), as well as quantitative and qualitative analyses of speech tokens (see \autoref{app:analysis}).
