\section{Intelligent Design 4.0: Agentic and Multi-Agent AI Systems}
\label{sec:sec6}

While LLM-driven and MLLM-driven methods presents advanced capabilities to design, a single foundation model remains insufficient to handle the complexity of the full stack of engineering design. The whole design process spans a wide range of cognitive, computational, domain-specific subtasks. The design methods and tools of ID 3.0 still require human oversight for task decomposition, context switching, and coordination \cite{Wang2025humanllm}. In addition, recent studies have also revealed intrinsic capability variations across different models \cite{Hu2024}. For instance, some LLMs such as Claude models excel at code generation, while others like GPT models demonstrate stronger performance in text generation \cite{OpenAI2025,Anthropic2025}. These observations suggest that the complex design tasks, are better served not by a single foundation model, but by an ensemble of specialized, collaborative AI agents, each fine-tuned or prompted for particular functions \cite{Durante2024}. Figure 5 illustrates a fundamental framework of an AI agent. Powered by LLMs or MLLMs, AI agents can be defined as autonomous systems that can perceive its environment, break down complex tasks, evoke tool uses, learn from interactions (with both human users or other agents), and take actions toward achieving defined or evolving goals \cite{Durante2024}.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=12cm]{FIG5.png}
	\caption{The fundamental framework of an AI Agent}
	\label{fig:fig5}
\end{figure}

Building upon the foundation of AI agents, the paradigm of ID 4.0 envisions multi-agent system composed of autonomous, design task-specialized agents that coordinate and collaborate to complete complex, end-to-end design processes. We propose a conceptual framework for implementing ID 4.0, as illustrated in Figure 6.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=16cm]{FIG6.png}
	\caption{The Multi-Agent-based ID 4.0 Paradigm}
	\label{fig:fig6}
\end{figure}

In Figure 6, the left panel outlines a five-stage engineering design workflow adapted from \cite{Beitz1996}: Task Clarification, Concept Design, Embodiment Design, Detailed Design, and Design Optimization. These stages represent a typical iterative design loop, where solutions are progressively refined and evaluated. The right panel maps this framework onto a multi-agent AI system, where each design stage is managed by a corresponding stage-level agent:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{\textsf{Requirement Analysis Agent}} interprets user inputs, extracts design goals and constraints, and decomposes high-level tasks into sub-problems for downstream agents.
  \item \textbf{\textsf{Concept Generation Agent}} explores early-stage solutions using ideation tools and creative reasoning, often invoking supporting agents like Brainstorming and Empathy agents for divergent thinking.
  \item \textbf{\textsf{Embodiment Generation Agent}} translates selected concepts into more concrete forms, coordinating with modeling and simulation agents to assess feasibility and functionality.
  \item \textbf{\textsf{Detail Modeling Agent}} refines the geometry and specifications of the design, producing manufacturable and parametric models in collaboration with supporting agents like CAD agents and design databases.
  \item \textbf{\textsf{Design Optimization Agent}} applies generative and simulation-based optimization to fine-tune performance against multiple objectives or constraints.
\end{itemize}

These stage-level design agents are not static modules, but interactive and adaptive components. Each stage-level agent may operate as an individual LLM/MLLM-powered agent or as a coordinated group of agents sharing the same objective. Throughout the design process, each agent can dynamically take the lead in decomposing tasks, triggering downstream agents, and coordinating others as needed. To support complex operations, each stage-level agent accesses a shared pool of functional agents that provide domain-general capabilities across the design workflow:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{\textsf{Web Search Agent}} retrieves up-to-date external knowledge and references from the internet.
  \item \textbf{\textsf{Database Agent}} accesses structured domain-specific datasets or internal databases to support data-informed decisions.
  \item \textbf{\textsf{Empathy Agent}} infers user intent and emotional context to align design proposals with users’ expectations.
  \item \textbf{\textsf{Brainstorming Agent}} facilitates divergent thinking to generate alternative concepts and creative directions.
  \item \textbf{\textsf{CAD Modeling Agent}} synthesizes 2D and 3D geometries and integrates parametric constraints.
  \item \textbf{\textsf{CAE Simulation Agent}} conducts multi-physics simulations (e.g., structural, thermal, and fluid) to evaluate functional performance of designs.
  \item \textbf{\textsf{Requirement Check Agent}} verifies compliance with user-defined goals, regulatory constraints, and quality criteria.
\end{itemize}

These functional agents are not merely passive tools like external softwires or APIs. They are also equipped with capabilities for planning, adaptation, and system information sharing, enabling more intelligent and coordinated collaboration with stage-level agents. While Figure 6 illustrates a representative set of agents, real-world design environments may incorporate additional or domain-specific agents to meet specialized functional needs.

The entire multi-agent system interacts with human designers (initiate tasks and receive design outputs) through a centralized communication interface. Within the system, all agents can access external tools and services via standardized protocols \cite{Anthropic2024}. Additionally, agents operate within a shared information environment, comprising both short-term and long-term memory, which support cross-agent coordination and cumulative learning throughout the design process.

This agentic framework enables iterative collaboration and distributed task execution across design stages. For example, the \textsf{Concept Generation Agent} may invoke the \textsf{Brainstorming Agent} and \textsf{Empathy Agent} to explore early-stage ideas, and then transfer selected candidates to the \textsf{CAD Modeling Agent} and \textsf{CAE Simulation Agent} for embodiment and performance evaluation. The \textsf{Requirement Check Agent} ensures that all design outputs remain aligned with user-defined goals and a range of desired system properties such as quality, reliability, and maintainability that often emerge during operational use \cite{Lai2006}. In recent, several studies have explored the application of AI agents in design practice, validating on engineering problems such as aerodynamic vehicle design and mechatronic system design \cite{Zhang2025designgpt,Chen2025multiagent,Elrefaie2025,Wang2025multiagent}. By reducing the dependence on human intervention for coordination and design decision-making, ID 4.0 has the potential to fundamentally transform engineering design creativity and productivity, enabling continuous and autonomous design processes without being limited by human time, effort, or expertise.

\section{Challenges and Opportunities Ahead for ID 4.0}
\label{sec:sec7}

As ID 4.0 evolves, its potential lies not only in automating existing design workflows, but in reimagining the nature of intelligence, autonomy, and collaboration in engineering. It brings a shift from static processes to dynamic ecosystems of learning, adaptation, and coordination. While recent studies have validated early prototypes of multi-agent design systems powered by foundation AI models \cite{Zhang2025designgpt,Chen2025multiagent,Elrefaie2025,Wang2025multiagent}, some important open questions remain, particularly regarding how such systems can be scaled, grounded, coordinated, and goal-setting in increasingly complex and practical design contexts.

\subsection*{(1) Toward More Complex Design: Multi-scale and Multi-physics Co-design}

As engineering design problems increase in complexity, they increasingly require integrated reasoning across disparate physical scales (e.g., nano to macro) and physics domains (e.g., structural, thermal, fluid, electromagnetic) \cite{DeBorst2008}. Complex products such as aerospace systems, robotics, and biomedical devices often involve tight coupling between microstructural properties, component-level geometries, control strategies, and performance in dynamic environments \cite{Zhao2022,Wang2025codesign,Wang2021}.

Traditional design methods often treat these physical domains independently, requiring manual iteration across disparate software tools and collaboration among multiple disciplinary teams. Within the ID 4.0 paradigm, specialized agents can be orchestrated to operate at different modeling scales, enabling coherent and system-level co-design. For example, a material-level simulation agent could inform a structural optimization agent via shared intermediate representations, facilitating more comprehensive performance integration.

Despite this potential, current foundation models still lack accurate embedded knowledge in fields such as nonlinear mechanics, heat transfer, or optics \cite{Shen2025}. Their reasoning is frequently statistical, not governed by physical laws. Closing this gap may require hybrid systems that integrate MLLM-powered agents with symbolic physics engines or world models \cite{Agarwal2025}. The advancement of such hybrid systems could yield cross-scale design agents capable of reasoning from atomic structures to macro-level artifacts within multi-physics design environments.

\subsection*{(2) Toward More Practical Design: Context-aware Design with Real-world Constraints}

While ID 4.0 have made significant achievements in generating functional and innovative design solutions, these outcomes must ultimately align with the practical realities of implementation. In real-world engineering, design decisions must account for a variety of non-ideal constraints, including manufacturing capabilities, supply chain limitations, material availability, cost structures, and environmental regulations \cite{Xiong2019,Gibson2021,Chen2010}. A design that performs optimally in simulation may still fail if it cannot be manufactured efficiently or sourced reliably. Moreover, design outputs, especially when conceived as complex systems, must consider a set of quality attributes (commonly referred to as “ilities” \cite{Bertoni2019}), such as reliability, maintainability, scalability, and quality. These attributes are critical to ensure that the system can deliver sustained value to stakeholders and users over its lifecycle \cite{Xie2004}.

To move toward more practically deployable design intelligence, ID 4.0 must become context-aware, embedding real-world data into the reasoning loop. This requires integrating agents with external systems or data sources such as manufacturing databases, supplier networks, market analytics platforms, and internal databases in organizations like Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) systems. Through such integration, agents can dynamically retrieve and process contextual information, such as material availability, production tolerances, cost trends, carbon footprints, and supply chain risks.

When multiple constraints conflict, design agents must balance trade-offs through principled methods. In this case, agents may involve multi-objective optimization to reason over competing criteria, such as cost vs. performance or speed vs. sustainability. These optimization components can function as internal capabilities or external tools invoked via standardized protocols.

\subsection*{(3) Toward More Effective Agent Collaboration}

Current research in multi-agent intelligent design systems often relies on a rigidly defined design process to orchestrate collaboration among agents \cite{Zhang2025designgpt}. While such structured workflows are valuable for feasibility studies and benchmarking, they may be limited in more open-ended design scenarios, where creative exploration and emergent strategy are essential.

To move beyond these limitations, ID 4.0 demands more flexible and dynamic coordination mechanisms, potentially inspired by concepts from self-organizing systems or multi-agent negotiation \cite{Zhu2025bench}. This remains an open question for design practice: how can design agent teams autonomously allocate tasks, exchange knowledge, and evolve their collaboration strategies effectively?

Furthermore, as foundation models continue to grow in capacity, the boundary between the multi-agent framework and the single “super-agents” may become blurred \cite{Yao2025}. This raises critical questions: At what point could a single model effectively manage the full stack of an end-to-end design workflow? And how should we balance the generality and power of such models against the modularity, interpretability, and fault isolation offered by multi-agent systems? These questions lie at the heart of the orchestration problem in ID 4.0: what is the optimal architecture for intelligent collaboration - distributed agents with explicit communication and specialization, or unified agents with general-purpose capabilities? Future research and practice must explore hybrid architectural strategies that can dynamically evolve with advancements in model capabilities, balancing the strengths of specialization and generality within intelligent design systems.

\subsection*{(4) Toward New Forms of Goal-setting}

Engineering design has long been a human-centered activity, with goals explicitly specified by users based on functional requirements, stakeholder needs, and contextual constraints \cite{Zhu2024}. Even under the ID 4.0 paradigm, human designers typically serve as the ultimate arbiters of what constitutes a desirable outcome \cite{Zhu2025}. However, as agentic AI systems grow more capable of reasoning, interacting, and learning from context, a new possibility emerges: Can AI agents begin to formulate goals themselves? For example, in response to vague or under-specified prompts, an ID 4.0 system might extrapolate user intent, identify broader system-level opportunities, or suggest higher-level objectives that were previously unconsidered.

The shift from human-defined goals to machine-generated goals may present a fundamental change in how design is conceived and directed \cite{Yang2025}. It challenges the long-standing assumption that human intention is the sole source of meaningful goals. If agents are empowered to redefine the design problem or elevate the abstraction level of design targets, they may unlock higher-order creativity and autonomous problem discovery. In doing so, ID 4.0 could evolve from a tool for goal execution into a partner in ideation, capable of augmenting or even co-driving the innovation process.

At the same time, the shift toward machine-defined goals raises fundamental ethical and legal questions \cite{Mukherjee2025}. Who is accountable for a machine-generated goal? How do we ensure that such goals respect cultural, environmental, or organizational norms? And what does authorship mean when systems contribute not only as problem-solvers but also as problem-setters?

To better align ID 4.0 systems with human values, we need alignment on two fronts: technically, through transparent reasoning processes and human-in-the-loop validation; and socially, by embedding ethical norms and stakeholder priorities into system design. In addition, relevant policies should ensure that system actions are traceable, responsibilities are well-defined, and human intervention remains possible when needed.

\section{Conclusion}

Intelligent Design 4.0 represents a critical inflection point in the ongoing evolution of design intelligence, shifting from data-driven assistance to multi-agent autonomy empowered by foundation AI models. In this paper, we reviewed the paradigm evolution of Intelligent Design through four developmental stages, and presented a multi-agent-based framework for ID 4.0. Beyond reviewing past developments, we have outlined future directions and open challenges toward a more scalable, practical, effective design paradigm. We hope this work offers both conceptual foundations and actionable perspectives to guide researchers, designers, and developers in advancing the next generation of intelligent design systems.

