\section{Conclusions}

Our integrated UAV prototype, centered on YOLO-based deer detection, an ACO coverage planner, a contact charging dock, and a reinforcement learning (RL) supervisor, has shown in simulation and small-plot trials that it can detect deer with 92\% accuracy, cover fields 15\% more efficiently than a baseline boustrophedon. In the coverage study, both Ant System (AS) and Max–Min AS (MMAS) outperformed the back-and-forth pattern; AS produced slightly shorter, more reliable tours, whereas MMAS more often converged to non-returning paths, mirroring the pheromone-update effect noted by Dorigo \textit{et al.} (\cite{dorigo_ant_2006}). The RL policy, trained in a photorealistic AirSim farm with procedurally animated deer, successfully coordinates detection, deterrence manoeuvres, and battery management, confirming that our modular interfaces allow learning to sit atop well-tested vision, planning, and charging subsystems. 

% When comparing the performance of AS and MMAS, AS generated paths with slightly better fuel efficiency on average. While both of these ACO algorithms produced similar results when comparing valid solutions–paths that return to the charging station–MMAS converged on invalid solutions more often than AS did. We suspect that this is because AS explores more potential paths than MMAS does since all ants deposit pheromones in AS, while only the best performing ant does so in MMAS~\cite{dorigo_ant_2006}. However, these findings are preliminary, and more testing needs to be conducted in different enviroment setups to have conclusive results.

% A key avenue for future research is the refinement of each subsystem to further enhance real-world robustness. On the path planning side, we plan to incorporate local search into our implementations of ACO to avoid converging to local maxima and conduct more rigorous testing and comparison with other coverage algorithms. Future work can explore allowing edges that cross obstacles to stay in the solution space by implementing collision avoidance that allows a UAV to depart from the straight-line path in order to safely get to a waypoint. With regards to computer vision, we plan to incorporate additional sensor modalities, such as thermal imaging, to improve deer detection during low-light conditions and further minimize false positives. Enhancements to the autonomous charging station—such as improved landing guidance or battery-swapping mechanisms—could also extend operational uptime.

Beyond farm settings, the generalizability of our framework invites applications in wildlife management, conservation, and disaster response. In all these scenarios, a UAV needs to monitor wide expanses and respond rapidly to emergent events (e.g., poaching incidents or spreading wildfires). Our focus on modularity and real-time adaptation provides a strong foundation for these broader deployments. Over the coming months, we plan to engage in more extensive field trials, scale our system to multi-UAV coordination, and deepen our collaboration with local farmers and wildlife experts. Through these steps, we aim to transform this early-stage prototype into a robust, multi-use platform with a tangible impact on sustainable agriculture and environmental stewardship.

