\section{Relative Strength}
\label{sec:expressivity}
Now that we have formally defined the various types of distributed knowledge that we are interested in, we can investigate their properties. In particular, we are interested in which variants imply each other.

%Among the properties we want to study is which of the variants are actually different.

In most cases, it is clear that one variant $\tau_1$ is at least as strong as another variant $\tau_2$, in the sense that $\M,s\models_{\tau_1}D_G\phi$ implies $\M,s\models_{\tau_2}D_G\phi$.
In particular, it is easy to see that the following hold.
\begin{proposition}\hfill
\label{prop:basic_comparisons}
\begin{itemize}
    \item For every $\tau$, $\M,s\models_\tau D_G\phi$ implies $\M,s\models_\nlall \rev{D_G}\phi$.

    % For every $\tau$, $\M,s\models_\tau D_G\phi$ implies $\M,s\models_{\nlsingle}D_G\phi$ and $\M,s\models_{\nlall}D_G\phi$.
    \item For every $f$, $a$ and $o$, $\M,s\models_{(f,a,o,\all)}D_G\phi$ implies $\M,s\models_{(f,a,o,\some)}D_G\phi$.
    % Holding all other parameters constant, the ``all'' variant implies the ``single'' variant.
    \item For all $o$ and $q$, $\M,s\models_{(\yeslang,\single,o,q)}D_G\phi$ implies $\M,s\models_{(\yeslang,\multi,o,q)}D_G\phi$.

    % Holding all other parameters constant, ``single'' implies ``set''.
    \item For all $a$ and $q$, $\M,s\models_{(\yeslang,a,\simult,q)}D_G\phi$ implies $\M,s\models_{(\yeslang,a,\seq,q)}D_G\phi$.

    Furthermore, $\M,s\models_{(\yeslang,\multi,\seq,q)}D_G\phi$ implies $\M,s\models_{(\yeslang,\multi,\ord,q)}D_G\phi$.
    % Holding all other parameters constant, ``simultaneous'' implies ``finite sequential'' (or ``$\omega$-sequential''), which in turn implies ``$\Omega$-sequential''.
\end{itemize}
\end{proposition}
Furthermore, it follows from \cite{vanderhoek99} that $\nlall$ and $\nlsingle$ do not imply $\formulasimall$.%\footnote{Technically, \cite{vanderhoek99} shows the results for a different definition of distributed knowledge where entailment plays a role, but through application of compactness and the deduction theorem, they can be seen to be equivalent.}
% (This is actually more complicated, which I will need to add later. \cite{vanderhoek99} uses a different notion for distributed knowledge, namely logical consequence. This makes no different for the ``one formula per agent'' case, but does for some other cases. Further distinction needed!)
This, however, leaves many comparisons open, which we solve here.

