%			We claim that the restriction of $H$ to the vertex set $A''_1 \cup \ldots \cup A''_{r-1} \cup T$ is a copy of $K_{t,t,\ldots,t}^r$ in $H$. Indeed, let $(a_1,\ldots,a_r)$ be an $r$-tuple such that $a_i \in A''_i$ for all $1 \leq i \leq r-1$ and $a_r \in T$. It is clearly sufficient to show that $a_1 \cap \ldots \cap a_{r-1} \subset a_r$. Note that $a_1$ participates in some $(r-1)$-tuple in $S$, say $a_1,a'_2,\ldots,a'_{r-1}$. By the definition of a Type~B intersection, $a_1$ contains $a_1'$. Furthermore,    


%			But now, we don't need all the clipping process, and by the induction hypothesis we obtain the $K_{t, \ldots,t}^{r-1}$ in $A_1 \cup \ldots \cup A_r$ that together with $T$ forms the desired $K_{t, \ldots,t}^{r}$.

		Let $Z=\bigcap\{a_r : a_r \in T\}$ be the intersection of all the pseudo-discs in $T$. Since all the elements of $T$ contain a common point (e.g., each point in $P$), $Z$ is non-empty and  connected. 

		Now, we clip each pseudo-disc $s \in A_1 \cup \ldots \cup A_{r-1}$ to $s'=s \cap Z$, and slightly perturb the boundaries such that the family $\{s': s \in  A_1 \cup \ldots \cup A_{r-1}\}$ is still a family of pseudo-discs.

		Consider the $(r-1)$-partite intersection hypergraph $H'$ of the families $A_1', \ldots,A'_{r-1}$, where $A_i'=\{s':s\in A_i \}$. $H'$ has at least $|S|$ hyperedges, since for each $(a_1,\ldots,a_{r-1}) \in S$, the point $p(a_1,\ldots,a_{r-1})$ is contained in $Z$, and hence, $a'_1 \cap a'_2 \cap \ldots \cap a'_{r-1} \neq \emptyset$. Since $|S|> C_{r-1} t^6 n^{r-2} (\log n)^{r-3}$, by the induction hypothesis $H'$ contains a $K_{t,\ldots,t}^{r-1}$ all of whose elements are fully contained in $Z=\bigcap\{a:a \in T\}$. Together with the set $T \subset A_r$, we obtain $K_{t,\ldots,t}^{r}$ in $H$.
