		\begin{remark} 
			The clipping process was needed in type-A but not in type-B. Indeed, in both cases we obtained $K_{t, \ldots,t}^{r-1}$ of pseudo-discs from $A_1 , \ldots , A_{r-1}$ all of whose elements intersect a $t$-tuple $T$ from $A_r$. But in type-B all the area of each $(r-1)$-intersection was contained in each pseudo-disc in $T$, therefore this $K_{t, \ldots,t}^{r-1}$ together with $T$ immediately implies the existence of $K_{t, \ldots,t}^{r}$. On the contrary, in type-A the $K_{t, \ldots,t}^{r-1}$  was not necessarily formed by the points $p(a_1, \ldots,a_{r-1})$ (but maybe by other points in the intersection that are not contained in all the elements of $T$). This is why we needed the trick of the clipping.
		\end{remark}
AT THIS POINT, IT IS NOT CLEAR TO US HOW TYPE B WORKS WITHOUT THE CLIPPING. IF CLIPPING IS INDEED NEEDED IN BOTH TYPES THEN THE PROOFS FOR BOTH TYPES ARE ALMOST IDENTICAL, WHICH LEADS TO WONDER WHETHER THE DIVISION INTO TYPES CAN BE AVOIDED.

