		\section{Lopsided Results for the Graph Zarankiewicz's Problem}\label{app:rect}

		In this appendix we prove Propositions~\ref{Prop:PtsBoxes} and~\ref{Prop:HorVer}, which give bounds for the lopsided Zarankiewicz problem for the intersection graphs of points and axis-parallel boxes in $\Re^d$ (Proposition~\ref{Prop:PtsBoxes}) and of horizontal and vertical segments in the plane (Proposition~\ref{Prop:HorVer}). Our proof strategy combines elements from~\cite{CH23,KellerS24}. In addition, we replace a geometric argument used in~\cite[Claim~3.2]{KellerS24} with a charging argument that yields a significantly better dependence on $t$.

%		\medskip

%		We begin with a sequence of propositions.
%		The first proposition, which improves a result of~\cite{KellerS24} and is essentially tight (see below), might be of independent interest.
		In the following, $G_{A,B}$ denotes the bipartite intersection graph of the families $A,B$.
		We begin with a lemma which improves a result of~\cite{KellerS24} and is essentially tight (see below). 
		\begin{definition}
			For a multiset $A$ of horizontal segments and for $t \in \mathbb{N}$, a $t$-tuple of segments in $A$ is called \emph{canonical} if there exists a vertical segment whose intersection with $A$ is exactly that tuple.
		\end{definition}

		\begin{lemma}\label{Prop:Canonical}
			For any multiset $A$ of horizontal segments and any $t \in \mathbb{N}$, the number of canonical $t$-tuples in $A$ is at most $(2t-1) |A|$.
		\end{lemma}

		\begin{proof}
			The proof is an easy charging argument. For each canonical $t$-tuple $I$, we take a vertical segment whose intersection with $A$ is exactly that tuple, and sweep it to the right (i.e., increasing the $x$ coordinate) until the intersection of the swept segment with $A$ is not equal to $I$ anymore. This can happen either immediately after a right endpoint of some $\ell_1 \in I$ (see Figure~\ref{fig:right-endpoint}) or at a left endpoint of some $\ell_0 \in A \setminus I$ (see Figure~\ref{fig:left-endpoint}). In the former case, we charge $I$ to $\ell_1$, and in the latter case we charge $I$ to $\ell_0$. (Note that the same $I$ can be charged to several different segments $\ell_0 \in A \setminus I$ and/or to one segment $\ell_1 \in I$. In such a case, we charge it to one of them chosen arbitrarily.) Clearly, each canonical $t$-tuple is charged to some $\ell \in A$. On the other hand, we claim that each $\ell \in A$ is charged at most $2t-1$ times. 

			\begin{figure}[ht]
				\begin{center}
					\scalebox{0.5}{
						\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{fig2.pdf}
					}
					\caption{An illustration for the proof of Proposition~\ref{Prop:Canonical}. The canonical $4$-tuple $(b_1,b_2,b_3,b_4)$ is charged to $b_2$. The swept line is $\ell$.}
					\label{fig:right-endpoint}
				\end{center}
			\end{figure}

			\begin{figure}[ht]
				\begin{center}
					\scalebox{0.5}{
						\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{fig3.pdf}
					}
					\caption{An illustration for the proof of Proposition~\ref{Prop:Canonical}. The canonical $4$-tuple $(b_1,b_2,b_3,b_4)$ is charged to $b_5$. The swept line is $\ell$.}
					\label{fig:left-endpoint}
				\end{center}
			\end{figure}

			Indeed, let $\ell \in A$ and denote the $x$-coordinates of its left and right endpoints by $x_L,x_R$, respectively. Let $\epsilon$ be sufficiently small, such that no $x$-coordinate of an endpoint of any $\ell' \in A$ lies in $[x_L-\epsilon,x_L)$. Observe that $\ell$ can be charged as a right endpoint of some canonical $t$-tuple $I$ only if among the segments of $A$ that intersect the vertical line $x=x_L-\epsilon$, $I$ is a consecutive $t$-tuple which contains $\ell$. The number of such consecutive $t$-tuples for any given line is at most $t$.    
			Similarly, $\ell$ can be charged as a left endpoint of some canonical $t$-tuple $I'$ only if $\ell \not \in I'$ and among the segments of $A$ that intersect the vertical line $x=x_R$, $I' \cup \ell$ is a consecutive $(t+1)$-tuple in which $\ell$ is not the first and not the last element. The number of such consecutive $(t+1)$-tuples for any given line is at most $t-1$.  

			Hence, each $\ell \in A$ is charged at most $t+(t-1)=2t-1$ times, and consequently, the number of canonical $t$-tuples is at most $(2t-1)|A|$, as asserted.
		\end{proof}
		Lemma~\ref{Prop:Canonical} improves over a bound of $O(t^5 |A|)$ that was proved in~\cite[Claim~3.2]{KellerS24} using a non-trivial planarity argument. It is tight up to a constant factor, as is demonstrated by the example depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:hourglass}. In the figure, each of the top $n-2t+1$ black segments in $A$ is the top segment of exactly $t$ canonical $t$-tuples, each of which consists of the segment itself, the $k-1$ segments just below it, and the $t-k$ top red segments (for each $k=1,2,\ldots,t$). Hence, the number of canonical tuples in $A$ is at least $(n-2t+1)t=\Omega(t |A|)$ for all $t \ll |A|/2$. 

