\begin{abstract} 
Linear logic ($\LL$) is a resource-aware, abstract logic programming language that refines both classical and intuitionistic logic. 
Linear logic semantics is typically presented in one of two ways: by associating each formula with the set of all contexts that can be used to prove it (\eg\ phase semantics) or by assigning meaning directly to proofs (\eg\ coherence spaces). 

This work proposes a different perspective on assigning meaning to proofs by adopting a proof-theoretic perspective. %, where meaning is grounded in {\em proofs}.
%rather than {\em truth}. 
%\red{it seems like there isn't much contrast compared to the semantics listed as they already emphasise ``prove"/``proofs'', should we make the contrast truth/proofs more obvious somehow?}
More specifically, we employ base-extension semantics ($\Bes$) to characterise proofs through the notion of base support.
%\red{[}While proof-theoretic semantics (PtS) treats proofs and derivations as fundamental semantic concepts, base-extension semantics ($\Bes$)--one of the most prominent strands of PtS--characterizes proofs through a notion of base support. In this framework, an atom is supported by a base--\ie\ a set of inference rules with atomic premises and conclusions--if and only if it is derivable within it. Non-atomic formulas are supported if the base satisfies semantic conditions that determine what qualifies as a proof of that formula.\red{] i would remove this part completely - too many details for an abstract and then what this paper focuses on becomes like 5\% of the abstract which is not informative}
%\cyan{I liked the new title very much but I'm not sure of the best way to shorten the abstract (I agree it's too long for our current purposes). Katya's suggestion of removing the last part seems reasonable to me, but if the panel doesn't know much about PtS it might also be a good idea to provide a (perhaps shorter?) explanation of what is PtS and $\Bes$. In any case, will leave it to you both to decide what to keep and what to throw away!} \red{this is what the introduction is for}
Recent developments have shown that $\Bes$ is powerful enough to capture proof-theoretic notions in structurally rich logics such as intuitionistic linear logic. In this paper, we extend this framework to the classical case, presenting a proof-theoretic approach to the semantics of the multiplicative-additive fragment of linear logic ($\MALL$).

%Proof-theoretic semantics (PtS) provides an alternative to model-theoretic semantics by regarding proofs and derivations as core semantic concepts. Base-extension semantics ($\Bes$), one of the most prominent strands of PtS, characterizes proofs semantically through a notion of base support, in which an atom is supported by a base (\textit{i.e.} a set of natural deduction rules with atomic premises and conclusion) just in case it is derivable in it, and non-atomic formulas are supported just in case the base satisfies semantic conditions representing what counts as a proof of that formula. As shown in recent developments, this framework is even powerful enough to capture proof notions of logics as structurally rich as Intuitionistic Linear Logic.  $\Bes$ exhibits properties expected of a properly semantic framework and at times behaves similarly to its model-theoretic counterpart, which is essentially due to the fact that its proof-theoretic structure enables the demonstration of semantic results through syntactic techniques. In this paper we show another interesting connection between the semantic characterisation of proofs and model-theoretic truth conditions: just like classical models can sometimes be obtained by restricting intuitionistic models, classical proof conditions can be obtained through a very small, uniform restriction on intuitionistic proof conditions (namely: we require the production of a proof of $\bot$, a fixed atom, in all cases where we previously required the production of a proof of an arbitrary atom). In logics where $\bot$ is explosive it is also necessary to require bases to contain atomic rules encapsulating the \textit{ex falso sequitur quodlibet} principle, but otherwise $\bot$ does not need to have any special properties other than that of being a fixed atom. The restriction is as simple as it is illuminating, and it clarifies the semantic import of atomic quantification as well as its relation to structural operations. We show that the restriction works even in cases as complex as that of Linear Logic, and our main technical result is that by restricting the proof conditions of Multiplicative Additive Intuitionistic Linear Logic we obtain sound and complete semantics for Multiplicative Additive (Classical) Linear Logic.

%The proof-theoretic structure of $\Bes$ enables the demonstration of semantic result through syntactic techniques, so it retains the properties expected of a properly semantic framework and exhibits some behaviors that are quite similar to its model-theoretic counterparts.
