\section{Methodology}
\label{Methodology}
\subsection{Stochastic Game Formulation}
To capture the dynamic and uncertain nature of the operational environment and the interdependent decision-making processes of individual UAVs, the multi-UAV networking problem is modeled as a stochastic game defined by the tuple $(\boldsymbol{\mathcal{U}}, \boldsymbol{\mathcal{S}}, \{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{A}}^u\}_{u\in\boldsymbol{\mathcal{U}}}, P, \{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{R}}^u\}_{u\in\boldsymbol{\mathcal{U}}}, \gamma)$.

% In this paper, we formulate the networking task as a Stochastic Game with multiple agents, defined by $(\mathcal{U}, \boldsymbol{\mathcal{S}}, \{A_u\}_{u\in\mathcal{U}}, P, \{R_u\}_{u\in\mathcal{U}}, \gamma)$. This formulation is a generalization of the Markov Decision Process to multiple agents, allowing for a more natural representation of the interactions between agents operating in a shared environment.
\subsubsection{State space $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{S}}$}
The state space of the networking environment is defined to encompass a) the spatial coordinates of all nodes within the network, b) the status of communication links, including SNR and data rate, c) the connectivity status of each UAV to the available BS via the multi-hop network, denoted by $\{c_u^\text{UAV}(t)\}_{u\in\boldsymbol{\mathcal{U}}}$.

% It defines a set of all possible states of the whole environment. In the networking scenario, the environment state at time step $t$ consists of a) the positions of BSs, UAVs and UEs, b) the status of the communication links among these nodes, c) the maximum communication data rate that a UE can achieve and d) the metric $\{c_u^{UAV}(t)\}_{u\in\mathcal{U}}$ indicating whether a UAV can connect to the BSs directly or through multiple hops via the network.

\subsubsection{Action space $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{A}}=\{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{A}}^u\}_{u\in\boldsymbol{\mathcal{U}}}$}
It defines all possible UAV actions, encompassing both movement direction and speed. This paper assumes that UAVs operate at a fixed speed $\omega$ and their movement is confined to the planar domain, including eight discrete directional movements along with a hovering option.


% $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{A}}=\{A_u\}_{u\in\mathcal{U}}$ defines the possible UAV actions. For each UAV $u$, action $a_u(t)\in A_u$ include its moving direction and speed at time step $t$. In this paper, we assume each UAV $u$ moves at a constant speed $\omega$, with movement directions including forward, backward, left, right, four diagonal directions, ascending, descending, hovering, totaling eleven directions.

\subsubsection{State transition probability $P$}
The state transition defines how the system evolves based on UAV actions and environmental dynamics. Given the current state $s_t$, each UAV $u$ selects an action $a_t^u$, leading to a new state $s_{t+1}$ with probability $P(s_{t+1}|s_t,\{a_t^u\}_{u\in\boldsymbol{\mathcal{U}}})$.

% It determines the probability distribution over next states $s'$ given the current state $s$ and the actions taken by all UAVs, which is defined as $P(s'|s,\{a_v\}_{v=1}^{U})$. The transition probability models the dynamics of the environment, incorporating the effects of all UAVs' actions.

\subsubsection{Reward model $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{R}}=\{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{R}}^u\}_{u\in\boldsymbol{\mathcal{U}}}$}
The reward model $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{R}}$ quantifies UAV actions by evaluating network connectivity and data rate. Accordingly, the team reward, representing the environmental feedback on the effectiveness of UAV networking, is defined as
\begin{equation}
    R_t=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1} ^M c_m^\text{UE}(t) + \frac{\kappa}{M}\sum_{m=1} ^M r^\text{UE}_m(t).
    \label{team_rew}
\end{equation}
