To ensure efficient policy training and model scalability, UAVs are grouped according to their specific roles in the networking task, shown in Figure \ref{group method}. Within the networking environment, some UAVs are initially positioned closer to the BSs, making them well-suited for data relay functions. Conversely, some UAVs are located nearer to the UEs yet remain relatively distant from available BSs in emergency conditions, thereby necessitating robust communication links with users. Additionally, certain UAVs may be required to balance both responsibilities. Consequently, the responsibilities and training objectives of different UAV agents vary. To address this heterogeneity, the UAVs are partitioned into different groups $\mathds{G}_i,i\in\{1,2,\dots,N_\mathds{G}\}$.
% The number of UAVs in each group is determined by the number of BSs and the size of the environment. 
Specifically, each UAV $u$ is assigned to a group based on its distance to the nearest BS at time step 0, defined as
\begin{equation}
d_u^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{G}}}=\text{min}\{d_{u,g}(t=0)|g\in\boldsymbol{\mathcal{G}}\}.
\label{grouping_dist}
\end{equation}
% all UAVs are first sorted in ascending order according to their corresponding $d_u^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{G}}}$ values. A 
To enable efficient group partitioning,
a quantile-based segmentation strategy is applied to divide them into multiple groups.
% Each group is defined by the range of $ d_u^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{G}}} $ values between the $\left( \frac{i-1}{N_\mathds{G}} \right)$-th and $\left( \frac{i}{N_\mathds{G}} \right)$-th quantiles. The $p$-th quantile of the distribution is denoted by $ Q_p $, and we have
% \begin{equation}
%     \mathds{G}_i = \left\{ u \;\middle|\; Q_{\frac{i-1}{N_\mathds{G}}} \leq d_u^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{G}}} < Q_{\frac{i}{N_\mathds{G}}} \right\},  u\in\boldsymbol{\mathcal{U}}, i\in\{1,2,\dots,N_\mathds{G}\}.
% \end{equation}
In this strategy, the UAVs are sorted based on $d_u^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{G}}}$ and then partitioned into groups by dividing the ordered list into quantiles.
The number of groups and the size of each group are jointly determined by the total number of UAVs and the spatial scale of the environment.
UAVs with smaller $ d_u^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{G}}} $, typically located closer to BSs, are assigned to smaller groups, with group sizes approximately matching the number of BSs. In contrast, UAVs with larger $ d_u^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{G}}} $ values are grouped into larger subsets to improve communication coverage for UEs.
