\subsection{Results}


\paragraph{LLM-Generated \textit{v.s} LLM-Revised}  Tables~\ref{tab:halstead_stats} and~\ref{tab:maintainability_stats} present the results containing direct generation and reference-guided generation. As shown in the tables, for the same model in Python, the Halstead volume and effort of the solution obtained using the accepted (AC) code as the reference (REF) are generally lower than those generated directly (ANS). For example, the volume (195.18) and effort (1345.86) of Deepseek-REF-Python are lower than those of Deepseek-ANS-Python (volume: 201.03, effort: 1472.81). But the opposite trend is observed in C++, suggesting that \textbf{for Python, reference-guided generation leads to more concise code, whereas in C++, direct generation yields more concise results}.


\paragraph{LLM \textit{v.s} Human} 
Figure~\ref{error_bar_cyc} illustrates the cyclomatic complexity of the code written by human and different models. Compared to code generated by various LLMs, the cyclomatic complexity of human-written C/C++ code ranks second highest. Figure~\ref{error_bar_py_sloc} compares the source lines of code (SLOC) for both human- and model-generated code, which means that human-written code also tends to have longer source lines of code (in both Python and C/C++) and that LLM-generated code is more concise. More results can be found in Tables~\ref{tab:halstead_status_subset} and~\ref{tab:maintainability_status_subset}.

\paragraph{Compared with the GitHub Dataset.} Figure~\ref{metrics_github_figure} shows the trend of the cyclomatic index and the SLOC index in the GitHub dataset. We find that there is no clear trend for either index over time, which may indicate that LLM is still limited in its role in improving code maintainability in complex scenarios such as GitHub repositories. In addition, Figure~\ref{git_cyclo} shows that the cyclomatic complexity of the CS category is always significantly higher than that of the non-CS category. Figure~\ref{git_sloc} indicates that the SLOC values for the two categories are relatively similar.

\finding{LLM's code writing has lower complexity and higher maintainability than humans in the scenario of IO algorithm problems. At the same time, the output is stable, and its rewritten code indicators are inferior to direct generation. 
}
