We consider a standard inverse kinematics (IK) task, where the objective is to compute a collision-free configuration of a robot manipulator such that the end-effector reaches a desired target pose. This corresponds to a single-stage, one-step optimization problem (i.e., $T = 1$ and $K = 1$ in \eqref{eq:general_opt}) with continuous decision variables $\bm{u}$, subject to kinematic and collision constraints. The objective function minimizes task-space error, which penalizes deviations between the end-effector pose and the desired target. Additional constraints include joint limits, collision avoidance, and reachability. Despite its simple definition, this task serves as a meaningful testbed featuring nonlinear kinematics and nonconvex constraints, making it well-suited to evaluate the effectiveness of TT in enabling efficient tree search.

We conducted ablation study to analyze the necessity of each component of TTTS, which consists of three main components: TT-Tree Initialization, TT-Tree Search, and TT-Tree Refinement. TT-Tree Initialization typically requires some computation time due to TT-Cross approximation (particularly for high-dimensional systems), but this process is performed offline. At this step, the state space is augmented with task variables, enabling rapid task-conditioned retrieval for TT-Tree Search. In our experiments, we set $r_{\max} = 21$ for both 3-DOF and 7-DOF manipulators, resulting in a coarse approximation of the full decision tree. Notably, obstacle avoidance tends to introduce high-rank behavior, making low-rank TT approximations less accurate. As shown in Figure \ref{fig:ablation_study} (A), TT-Tree Initialization alone does not yield the global optimum, highlighting the limitations of using TT approximation by itself. However, after performing TT-Tree Search and Refinement, the final task-space error is zero, indicating that the globally optimal solution was successfully found.


% We further compare our proposed approach with state-of-the-art baselines to demonstrate the efficiency, including TTGO, MCTS and CMA-ES. TTGO can be actually seen as a special case of our proposed approach TTTS. The vanilla TTGO naively sample the solutions based on TT approximation, where the same solution can be repeatedly sampled, while our proposed approach adopts the strategical search capability from MCTS, which enables the global convergence capability. Moreover, in our implementation of TTGO, we used gradient-based solver in scipy for solution refinement, which aligns well with the implementation of TTGO in \cite{shetty2016tensor}. Table \ref{tab:compa_sota} shows the comparison between our approach and the baselines. Except TTGO, all other methods find the global optimal solutions. It is because we only sample once in TTGO. Since low-rank approximation is not very precise, one iteration might not able to reach the global optimal solution. Given more iterations, TTGO is also possible to obtain the global optima, since the algorithm itself has probabilistic completenessness given infinite time, while TTTS utilizes strategical search, enabling global convergence within finite time. Moreover, the CMA-ES refinement embedded in TTTS also faciliates solution finding compared with gradient-desent solver used in TTGO, to address the non-convexity issue commonly existed in robot optimization problems. Among the three approaches reaching the global optima, TTTS requires least time, indicating the effectiveness of TT approximation for efficient tree search.



