% \begin{figure}[htbp]
%   \centering
%   \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{images/tree_tensor.png}
%   \caption{\textbf{Tree-Tensor Equivalence.} A multi-layer decision tree can be represented as a high-dimensional tensor, where each tensor element corresponds to the value at the terminal node of a branch.}
%   \label{fig:tree_tensor}
% \end{figure}

We introduce TT as an efficient representation for decision trees that significantly reduces combinatorial complexity to linear complexity. A multi-layer decision tree can be represented as a high-dimensional tensor $\mathbf{T}$, where each complete branch corresponds to a particular element within $\mathbf{T}$, and the element indices correspond to the sequence of node indices along that branch. As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:tree_tensor} (A), the two representations are equivalent. For instance, a decision tree with $d$ layers and $N$ nodes per layer corresponds to a tensor of size $N^d$. If the optimal solution in the tree is indexed by $(i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_d)$, where $i_k$ denotes the node index at the $k$-th layer, then the corresponding optimal tensor entry has the same index.

To reduce this combinatorial complexity, our objective is to express the tree with linear complexity $\mathcal{O}(\lambda N d)$, where $\lambda$ is a scaling factor. This is equivalent to representing a high-dimensional tensor of size $\mathcal{O}(N^d)$ using a compact, low-rank decomposition, which is a well-studied problem in numerical mathematics \cite{cichocki2016tensor}. Specifically, we employ TT to exploit the separable structure and reduce inter-layer dependencies. As a result, the original high-dimensional tensor can be efficiently represented using significantly fewer parameters through a sequence of third-order cores, namely:
\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:tt_rep}
	\bm{\mathbf{T}}_{(i_{1},\ldots,i_{d})} \approx \bm{\mc{T}}^1_{:,i_1,:}\bm{\mc{T}}^2_{:,i_2,:}\cdots \bm{\mc{T}}^d_{:,i_d,:},
\end{equation}
where $\bm{\mc{T}}^j$ is the core corresponding to the $j$-th dimension (i.e., the $j$-th layer of the tree). Figure \ref{fig:tree_tensor} (B) presents an illustrative example of a three-dimensional tensor.



% \begin{figure}[htbp]
%   \centering
%   \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{images/tt_3D.jpg}
%   \caption{\textbf{An illustrative example of a 3-dimensional tensor}. A 3-dimensional tensor can be represented using three third-order TT cores. The tensor element at index $(i_1, i_2, i_3)$ can be accessed by multiplying the corresponding matrix slices from the TT cores.}
%   \label{fig:tt_format}
% \end{figure}

Algorithm \ref{alg:TTTS} presents the pseudocode of the proposed method. As indicated in \eqref{eq:ucb}, a key component of MCTS is its ability to perform strategic search by leveraging the value and visit count information stored at each node in the tree, denoted by $\bm{Q}$ and $\bm{V}$, respectively. We utilize TT to efficiently store this information, represented as $\bm{\mc{Q}}$ and $\bm{\mc{V}}$.

To initialize the search, TT-Cross is first employed to approximate the decision tree using a low-rank TT model, augmented with conditional variables (e.g., task parameters or initial states), resulting in a conditional model $\widetilde{\bm{\mc{Q}}}$. Given a set of condition variables $\bm{z}$, the value tensor is instantiated as $\bm{\mc{Q}} = \widetilde{\bm{\mc{Q}}}(\bm{z})$. Owing to the \emph{maximum volume} principle used in TT-Cross, the resulting TT model typically captures representative features of the decision tree, enabling the identification of promising branches from the very first iteration. The visit count tensor $\bm{\mc{V}}$ is initialized to zero.

The algorithm then follows the standard MCTS pipeline: selection, expansion, simulation, and backpropagation. Due to dependence on the parent nodes, the value and visitation statistics of a node are path-dependent, introducing non-Markovian behavior. Querying the value of node $n_{i_{[j]}}$ requires summing over all completions of the branch from layer $j+1$ to depth $d$. Given the full tree approximated in TT format, the value of a node at level \( j \), denoted as \( q_{i_{[j]}} \), can be efficiently computed by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    q_{i_{[j]}} &= \sum_{i_{j+1}=0}^{N_{j+1}} \cdots \sum_{i_d=0}^{N_d} \bm{Q}_{(i_1, \ldots, i_j, i_{j+1}, \ldots, i_d)} \\
    &\approx \sum_{i_{j+1}=0}^{N_{j+1}} \cdots \sum_{i_d=0}^{N_d} \bm{\mc{Q}}^1_{:,i_1,:} \cdots \bm{\mc{Q}}^j_{:,i_j,:} \cdots \bm{\mc{Q}}^d_{:,i_d,:},
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:w_compute}
\end{equation}
where each TT core \( \bm{\mc{Q}}^l \) encodes the factorized structure of the tree at layer \( l \). To compute the node value at depth \( j \), we extract the corresponding slices from the first \( j \) TT cores (representing the visited layers), and perform summation over indices in the remaining cores from \( j+1 \) to \( d \). The final node value is obtained by multiplying the resulting matrices across all layers. Figure \ref{fig:tree_tensor} (C) illustrates how to compute the value of a node in the first layer of a three-layer tree using TT cores. The multiple arrows between TT cores indicate the ability to perform parallel selection in the TT-Tree. 

Similarly, the visit count $v_{i_{[j]}}$ is computed as:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    v_{i_{[j]}} &= \sum_{i_{j+1}=0}^{N_{j+1}} \cdots \sum_{i_d=0}^{N_d} \bm{V}_{(i_1, \ldots, i_j, i_{j+1}, \ldots, i_d)} \\
    &\approx \sum_{i_{j+1}=0}^{N_{j+1}} \cdots \sum_{i_d=0}^{N_d} \bm{\mc{V}}^1_{:,i_1,:} \cdots \bm{\mc{V}}^j_{:,i_j,:} \cdots \bm{\mc{V}}^d_{:,i_d,:}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:v_compute}
\end{equation}

Given $q_{i_{[j]}}$, $v_{i_{[j]}}$, and $v_{i_{[j-1]}}$, node expansion follows the UCB rule from \eqref{eq:ucb}. The TT-based representation enables parallel selection, which is typically non-trivial in node-based or table-based implementations. If no further expansion is possible, the current branch terminates. Otherwise, the branch is extended by one layer and followed by a simulation to the leaf. Rather than performing a random rollout, we leverage the TT model $\bm{\mc{Q}}$ as a global approximation of the decision tree to guide the simulation strategy via stochastic sampling, treating TT values as unnormalized probabilities. This enables parallel simulation and yields a top-$k$ set of candidate solutions. After the simulation phase, optimal paths and their corresponding indices are identified, followed by backpropagation to update $\bm{\mc{Q}}$ and $\bm{\mc{V}}$ in preparation for the next iteration. During backpropagation, the visit count model $\bm{\mc{V}}$ is updated using a residual strategy. Since the newly visited indices are known, we adapt the standard TT-Cross algorithm into a guided version, where the residual tensor $\Delta \bm{V}\gamma^l$ is approximated using only one iteration over the specified index set $i_{[\gamma]}$ at layer $\gamma$. The value model $\bm{\mc{Q}}$ can also be updated in a similar manner to enhance approximation accuracy, but this step can be omitted in practice to reduced runtime. In each iteration, a solution list $\mathcal{S}$ is refreshed to maintain the top-$k$ candidates. Our framework supports full parallelization across all MCTS components, unlike traditional MCTS methods, which typically allow only partial parallelization at the simulation stage \cite{chaslot2008parallel}. This significantly improves the convergence rate. Finally, the candidates obtained from TT-Tree Search are further refined using CMA-ES to correct for discretization errors in the continuous domain.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\scalebox{0.9}{
\caption{Tensor Train Tree Search (TTTS)}
\label{alg:TTTS}
\begin{minipage}{1.25\textwidth} % 1.25 to compensate for scaling
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{TensorTrainTreeSearch}{$\bm{x}_0$, $\bm{z}$, $\mathbf{\bm{\widetilde{Q}}}$, $J$, $L$, $\Omega_w$, $\mathcal{I}$}
\State {\textbf{Input: }{Initial state $\bm{x}_0$, Condition var. $\bm{z}$, Augmented decision tree $\mathbf{\bm{\widetilde{Q}}}$, Objective function $J$, \\ 
\quad \quad Maximum iteration $L$, Domain $\Omega_w = \{(w^{i_1}_1,\ldots, w^{i_d}_d): i_k \in \{1,\ldots,N_k\} \}$, \\ 
\quad \quad Index set $\mathcal{I} \subseteq \{1,\ldots,N_1\} \times \cdots \times \{1,\ldots,N_d\}$}}
\State {\textbf{Hyperparameters:} {Number of solutions $k$, Exploration param. $c$} \textcolor{gray}{\# default: $k=10$, $c=3$}}
\State{\textbf{Output: }{Top-$k$ solution list $\mathcal{S} $}}

\State {\color{gray}\textbf{// ===== TT-Tree Initialization =====}}
\State $\widetilde{\bm{\mc{Q}}} = \text{TT-Cross}(\mathbf{\bm{\widetilde{Q}}})$, $\bm{\mc{Q}} \leftarrow \widetilde{\bm{\mc{Q}}} (\bm{z})$

\State {\color{gray}\textbf{// ===== TT-Tree Search =====}}
\State $i_0 \gets \text{Node}(\bm{x}_0)$, $\bm{\mc{V}_0} \leftarrow \bm{0}$, $\mathcal{S} = [\;]$
\For{$\ell = 1, 2, \ldots$, L}
    \For{$j = 1, \ldots, d$}
        \State $q_{i_{[j]}} \leftarrow $ Eq. \eqref{eq:w_compute}, $v_{i_{[j]}}, v_{i_{[j-1]}} \leftarrow $ Eq. \eqref{eq:v_compute}
        \State $i_{[j]} \gets \arg\max\nolimits^k_{i_{[j]} \in \Omega_w} \left( \frac{q_{i_{[j]}}}{v_{i_{[j]}}} + c \cdot \sqrt{\frac{\log v_{i_{[j-1]}}}{v_{i_{[j]}}}} \right)$
        \State \textbf{if} $i_j$ is not expanded \textbf{then} \textbf{break}
    \EndFor

    \For{$s = j+1, \ldots, d$}
        \State $q_{i_{[s]}} \gets $ Eq. \eqref{eq:w_compute}
        \State $i_{[s]} \gets  \arg\max_{i \in \Omega}^{k_s}  q_{i_{[s]}}$
    \EndFor

    \State $\mathcal{W} \gets \Omega_w(i_{[d]})$
    \State $i_{[d]}^\star \gets \arg\max\nolimits^k_{i \in \mathcal{I}} J(\mathcal{W}_i)$
    \State $\bm{w}^\star  \gets \arg\max\nolimits^k_{\bm{w} \in \mathcal{W}} J(\bm{w})$

    \For{$\gamma = 1, \ldots, j$}
        \State $\bm{\mc{V}}_{\gamma}^l = \bm{\mc{V}}_{\gamma}^{l-1} + \text{Guided-Cross}(\Delta \bm{V}_\gamma^{l}, i_{[\gamma]})$
    \EndFor

    \State $\mathcal{S}_{aug} \gets \text{append}(\mathcal{S}, \bm{w}^\star)$
    \State $\mathcal{S} \gets \arg\max\nolimits^k_{\bm{w} \in \mathcal{S}_{aug}} J(\bm{w})$
\EndFor
\State {\color{gray}\textbf{// ===== TT-Tree Refinement =====}}
\State $\mathcal{S} \gets \text{CMA-ES}(\mathcal{S})$
\State \Return $\mathcal{S}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{minipage}
}
\end{algorithm}



\subsection*{Theoretical proof}

Since a decision tree can be equivalently represented as a tensor, we define a decision tree as \emph{low-rank} if its corresponding tensor is low-rank. A tensor is considered low-rank if it can be well approximated using a decomposition format such as CP, Tucker, or TT \cite{grasedyck2013literature}, where the ranks are significantly smaller than the original tensor dimensions. For example, in the TT format, a tensor is low-rank if the TT ranks of the third-order cores satisfy $r_j \ll N_j$ for all $j$.


Here, we present three theoretical results to show that TTTS can efficiently converge to a bounded-error solution of the optimization problem defined in \eqref{eq:general_opt}. First, we show that the bounded global optimum of a TT model can be retrieved efficiently with linear complexity. For decision trees that are low-rank, a TT model can be accurately constructed and yields a near-global solution due to its low approximation error. In the case of high-rank trees, TT approximation may incur larger errors. However, thanks to the exploration-exploitation mechanism of MCTS, the algorithm guarantees asymptotic convergence to the global optimum. Moreover, the TT representation enhances the search efficiency by guiding exploration and enabling parallel MCTS rollouts, significantly improving the convergence rate.




% Here, we theoretically demonstrate the global convergence of low-rank tree, and the asymptotic global convergence of high-rank tree.

% \subsubsection*{Global optimization of TT model}

\begin{theorem} Given a TT model $\bm{\mathcal{T}}$, the global optimum can be efficiently retrieved with linear time complexity.
\label{thm:TT-global-opt}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}

Given a TT representation, the value of any specific element can be accessed as:
\begin{align}
    &\bm{\mc{T}}(x_1, \dots, x_d) 
    = \sum_{\gamma_1=1}^{r_1} \sum_{\gamma_2=1}^{r_2} \cdots \sum_{\gamma_{d-1}=1}^{r_{d-1}} \notag \\
    &\quad \bm{\mc{T}}^1(1,\, x_1,\,\gamma_1)\bm{\mc{T}}^2(\gamma_1,\, x_2,\,\gamma_2) \cdots \bm{\mc{T}}^d(\gamma_{d-1},\, x_d,\,1),
    \label{eq:tt_element}
\end{align}
where each $\bm{\mc{T}}^k$ is a TT core of size $r_{k-1} \times N_k \times r_k$, with $N_k$ denoting the number of discretization points for $x_k$, and $r_k$ representing the TT ranks, which are small for low-rank trees.

This shows that each element of $\bm{\mc{T}}$ can be expressed as a finite sum of products of separable TT cores. As a result, the global optimum of $\bm{\mc{T}}$ can be found through a dimension-wise optimization procedure that does not require convexity or differentiability. This process is analogous to traversing a fully evaluated tree: by selecting the node with the highest value at each layer, the optimal branch can be identified.

Specifically, we carry out this process in TT format. As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:tree_tensor}, a decision tree can be equivalently represented as a tensor, which can then be expressed in TT format for efficient computation. Based on \eqref{eq:w_compute}, we compute the node values for each layer and choose the one with the highest value. By recursively traversing from the root to the final layer, the global optimum of the TT model is obtained, with a linear time complexity $\mathcal{O}(Ndr^2)$.

% To find the global optimal solution $\bm{T}_{i^*_{[j]}}$ given $\bm{T}$ in TT format, we can simply 

\end{proof}


\begin{theorem}
    Consider a high-dimension tensor $\mathbf{T}$ that is approximated by a low-rank TT model $\bm{\mc{T}}$, with approximation error $\epsilon$, the error of the found solution compared with the maximum value of $\mathbf{T}$ is bounded with $2\epsilon$, namely

    \begin{equation}
        \|\max \mathbf{T} - \mathbf{T}(\arg\max \bm{\mc{T}}) \| \leq 2\epsilon
    \end{equation}

\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Consider a discrete search space 
\(
  \Omega
\),
with \(\lvert\Omega\rvert < \infty\). We aim to find
\[
  \bm{x}^* = \arg\max_{\bm{x}\in\Omega} \mathbf{T}(\bm{x}),
  \quad
   \mathbf{T}^* = \max_{x\in\Omega}  \mathbf{T}(x).
\]
Suppose we first approximate $\mathbf{T}$ by a low-rank TT model $\bm{\mc{T}}$, satisfying
\[
  \|\bm{\mc{T}} - \mathbf{T} \|_{\infty} \;=\; \max_{x\in \Omega}\,|\bm{\mc{T}}(\bm{x}) - \mathbf{T}(\bm{x})| \;\le\; \epsilon.
\]
Then we can prove that 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
& \|\max \mathbf{T} - \mathbf{T}(\arg\max \bm{\mc{T}}) \| \\
 \leq\; & \| \max \mathbf{T} - \max \bm{\mc{T}} \| + \|\max \bm{\mc{T}} - \mathbf{T}(\arg\max \bm{\mc{T}})\| \\
 \leq\; & 2\epsilon
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\end{proof}

% \subsubsection*{High-rank Tensor Optimization}
\begin{theorem}
When the TT approximation error $\epsilon$ is non-negligible, TTTS retains the property of asymptotic global convergence, with TT representation significantly increasing the convergence speed.
\end{theorem}


\begin{proof}
In a MCTS framework (with a finite-depth tree corresponding to the coordinates of \(\bm{x}\in\Omega\)), we initialize each leaf node \(x\) with \(Q_0(\bm{x}) = \bm{\mc{Q}}(\bm{x})\). A standard UCB-based selection rule then balances exploitation (nodes with high \(Q\)) and exploration (nodes that are under-visited). 

\paragraph{Asymptotic Global Convergence.}
Because \(\Omega\) is finite, a classical result states that if every node is explored infinitely often (i.e., the algorithm does not permanently abandon any branch), MCTS converges almost surely to the global maximum:
\[
  \lim_{l\to\infty}\,
  \max_{\bm{x}\in \Omega} \; \bigl|\widehat{Q}_l(\bm{x}) - \mathbf{T}(\bm{x})\bigr| \;=\; 0, 
\]
hence $\arg\max_{\bm{x}}\,\widehat{Q}_l(\bm{x})\;\to\;\arg\max_{\bm{x}}\, \mathbf{T}(\bm{x})$, where \(\widehat{Q}_l\) is the node value estimate after \(l\) iterations.


\paragraph{TT Prior for Faster Convergence.}
A good TT approximation further \emph{accelerates} convergence in practice. If \(\bm{\mc{T}}\) is close to \(\mathbf{T}\) in \(\|\cdot\|_\infty\)-norm, the initial value assignments guide MCTS away from evidently suboptimal nodes. Indeed, let
\(\bm{x}^* \!= \arg\max \bm{\mc{T}}(\bm{x})\). Then
\[
  |\bm{\mc{T}}(\bm{x}^*) - \mathbf{T}(\bm{x}^*) | \leq \epsilon.
\]
Any node \(\bm{y}\) with \(\bm{\mc{T}}(\bm{y}) \le \bm{\mc{T}}^*-2\,\epsilon\) satisfies 
\(
   \mathbf{T}(\bm{y}) \le \bm{\mc{T}}(\bm{y})+\epsilon \le \bm{\mc{T}}^* - \epsilon \le \mathbf{T}(\bm{x}^*)
\),
so \(\bm{y}\) cannot exceed \(\bm{x}^*\) in true value. Hence, the UCB mechanism quickly allocates fewer visits to such nodes, reducing wasted exploration and sharpening the focus on genuinely near-optimal candidates. Mathematically, this effect appears in ``gap-based'' analyses for bandit algorithms, where an accurate prior \(\bm{\mc{T}}\) increases the effective gap of suboptimal nodes, thus lowering their exploration burden. Consequently, while MCTS still guarantees asymptotic global convergence, an accurate TT approximation substantially speeds up the reaching of near-optimal solutions in finite time.

\end{proof}



