It holds that the CN2 scheme endures a bigger time step than that of the BDF2 scheme, but the computation cost is still high comparing to the same scheme with a large time step, which motivates us to use the variable time-step scheme. The non-uniform time grids $0=t_0<t_1<\cdots<t_n<t_{n+1}<\cdots<t_{N_t}=T$ with the time steps $\tau_n=t_{n}-t_{n-1}$ for $1\leq n \leq N_t$ are used. Denote by $r_n=\tau_{n}/\tau_{n-1}$, the adjacent time-step ratio, and by $\nabla_{\tau}\bm{U}^n=\bm{U}^{n}-\bm{U}^{n-1}$, the difference operator. With the notations given above, the CN2 scheme and BDF2 \cite{ShenESIAM2022} scheme become:
\begin{sch}[VCN2]\label{sch:VFCN2}
	Given $\bm{U}^0\in [V_{ew};V_{ns}]$, and $\bm{U}^1\in [V_{ew};V_{ns}]$ is obtained using the fully discrete CN1 scheme, we compute $\bm{U}^{n+1}\in [V_{ew};V_{ns}]$ for $1\leq n \leq N_t-1$ via
	\begin{subequations}
		\begin{numcases}{} 
			\frac{\nabla_{\tau}\bm{U}^{n+1}}{\tau_{n+1}}-\nu \Delta_h \bm{U}^{n+\frac{1}{2}}+\bm{B}(\overline{\bm{U}}^{n+\frac{1}{2}},\bm{U}^{n+\frac{1}{2}})+\nabla_D P^{n+\frac{1}{2}}=\mathscr{F}^{n+\frac{1}{2}},\\[0.15cm]
			\nabla_d \cdot \bm{U}^{n+\frac{1}{2}}=\bm{0},
		\end{numcases}
	\end{subequations}
	where $\overline{\bm{U}}^{n+\frac{1}{2}}=(1+\frac{r_{n+1}}{2})\bm{U}^{n}-\frac{r_{n+1}}{2}\bm{U}^{n-1}$.
\end{sch}
\begin{sch}[VBDF2]\label{sch:VFBDF2}
	Given $\bm{U}^0 \in [V_{ew};V_{ns}]$, and $\bm{U}^1 \in [V_{ew};V_{ns}]$ is obtained using the fully discrete BDF1 scheme, we compute $\bm{U}^{n+1} \in [V_{ew};V_{ns}]$ for $1\leq n \leq N_t-1$ via
	\begin{subequations}
		\begin{numcases}{} 
			D_2\bm{U}^{n+1}-\nu \Delta_h \bm{U}^{n+1}+\bm{B}(\overline{\bm{U}}^{n+1},\bm{U}^{n+1})+\nabla_D P^{n+1}=\mathscr{F}^{n+1},\\[0.15cm]
			\nabla_d \cdot \bm{U}^{n+1}=\bm{0},
		\end{numcases}
	\end{subequations}
	where 
		\begin{align*}
D_2\bm{U}^{n+1}=\frac{1+2r_{n+1}}{\tau_{n+1} (1+r_{n+1})}\nabla_{\tau}\bm{U}^{n+1}-\frac{r_{n+1}^2}{ \tau_{n+1}(1+r_{n+1})}\nabla_{\tau}\bm{U}^n,
	\end{align*}
	and $\overline{\bm{U}}^{n+1}=(1+r_{n+1})\bm{U}^{n}-r_{n+1}\bm{U}^{n-1}$.
\end{sch}


In order to efficiently capture the dynamics of numerical solutions in different time scales, we give a variable time-step strategy \cite{QIAO2011SIAM} in this example. The variable time-step scheme based on the changes in energy between adjacent time level is used as follows:
\begin{align}
	\tau_{n+1}=\mathrm{max}
	\left\{  
	\tau_{\mathrm{min}}, \, \frac{\tau_{\mathrm{max}}}{\sqrt{1+\eta |\nabla_{\tau}E_h^n/\tau_n |^2}} 
	\right\},
\end{align}
where $\tau_{\mathrm{max}},~ \tau_{\mathrm{min}}$ denote the predetermined maximum and minimum time steps, as well as $\eta$ is chosen to be the user to adjust the level of adaptivity. Here, we choose $\tau_{\mathrm{max}}=10^{-1},~ \tau_{\mathrm{min}}=\frac{1}{120}$ and $\eta=4\times 10^{5}$. As we can see in Fig. \ref{energyev}, both VCN2 and VBDF2 schemes successfully obtain the energy curve similar to the exact energy curve, and the time step increases with the energy curve flattens out. In addition to that, we compare the computation cost and efficiency of the schemes with uniform time step and the variable time step in Table \ref{111}. It can be clearly seen from the table that the reduction in calculation time of the variable time-step scheme is very significant. Hence, the variable time-step scheme performs better than the uniform one.


