\begin{eqnarray}
& &\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny csw}}(X^\ell)}{\ell}-
\Delta_\ell(\alpha^{k},\alpha)-\epsilon_1(k)-\frac{\ell\log\alpha}{n}-\frac{\alpha^\ell}{n}\log \frac{n}{\ell}\nonumber\\
&\le&\frac{k}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}\rho_{\mbox{\tiny
LZ}}(x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})\nonumber\\
&\le&\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny
csw}}(X^m)}{m}+\frac{1}{m}+\epsilon_2(k,\alpha^{2m})+\frac{2(m+1)\alpha^{m+1}}{n}\log\frac{n}{m}.
\end{eqnarray}
then we must have
$n\gg\ell\gg k\gg m\gg 1$. The same inequalities apply for $\hH_{\mbox{\tiny
nob}}$. The same inequalities hold also with $\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny
csw}}(X^\ell)}{\ell}$ and $\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny csw}}(X^m)}{m}$ being
replaced by $\hH_{\mbox{\tiny csw}}(X_\ell|X^{\ell-1})$ and
$\hH_{\mbox{\tiny csw}}(X_m|X^{m-1})$, respectively.

We should also point out that for the purpose of upper bounding $\frac{k}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}\rho_{\mbox{\tiny
LZ}}(x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})$, we can also upper bound each term, $\rho_{\mbox{\tiny
LZ}}(x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})$, by the empirical entropy of $x_{ik+1}^{ik+k}$ using
Ziv's inequality, but the order $m$ of this empirical entropy must be much
less than $k$. For a given $m$, the redundancy term associated with Ziv's
inequality is of the order of $\frac{\log(\log k)}{\log k}$, whereas 
in the approach of comparison with the best $\alpha^m$-state machine, the
dominant redundancy term, $\epsilon_2(k,\alpha^m)$ is of the order of
$\frac{1}{\log k}$, and hence slightly better, but in the latter there is also
an additional term of $\frac{1}{m}$.
On the other hand, the above lower bound, whose main term is
$\hH_{\mbox{\tiny csw}}(X^\ell)/\ell$ (or $\hH_{\mbox{\tiny
nob}}(X^\ell)/\ell$) with $\ell\gg k$ can be essentially further lower bounded by the average
of the empirical entropies derived from blocks of length $q$ with $q\gg\ell$.
