\section*{Appendix - The Conditional Case}

Similarly as in \cite{ZL78}, we adopt the following model of finite-state
compression. A finite--state encoder is defined by a quintuplet
$$E=(\calX,\calY,\calU,\calZ,f,g),$$
where $\calX$ and $\calY$ are finite input alphabets of sizes $\alpha =
|\calX|$ and $\beta=|\calY|$, respectively,
$\calU$ is a finite set of variable-length binary strings, including possibly the empty string $\lambda$ (of length zero),
$\calZ$ is a finite set of states,
$f: \calZ \times \calX \times \calY\to\calU$ is the output function, and
$g: \calZ \times \calX\times\calY \to \calZ$ is the next-state function.
When infinite sequences, $\bx=x_1,x_2,\ldots$, $x_i\in\calX$,
$\by=y_1,y_2,\ldots$, $y_i\in\calY$,
$i=1,2,\ldots$, henceforth
referred to as the {\it source sequence} and the {\it side information
sequence}, are fed
into an encoder $E$, they produce an infinite output sequence
$\bu=u_1,u_2,\ldots$, $u_i\in\calU$, henceforth referred to as the {\it
compressed bit-stream},
while passing through an infinite
sequence of states
$\bz=z_1,z_2,\ldots$, $z_i\in\calZ$, according to
the following recursive equations, implemented for $i=1,2,\ldots$
\begin{eqnarray}
u_i&=&f(z_i,x_i,y_i) \label{ui}\\
z_{i+1}&=&g(z_i,x_i,y_i) \label{nextstate1}
\end{eqnarray}
where the initial state, $z_1$, is assumed fixed, and will be labeled
$z_\star$ hereafter. Here,
if $u_i=\lambda$, no output is produced at this
step, i.e., the system is idling and only the state evolves in response
to the input.

An encoder with $s$ states, or an $s$--state encoder,
$E$, is one with $|\calZ|=s$.
A few additional notation conventions, borrowed from \cite{ZL78}, will be convenient: By
$f(z_i,x_i^j,y_i^j)$, ($i\le j$) we refer
to the vector $u_i^j$ produced by $E$ in response to the input $(x_i^j,y_i^j)$
when the initial
state is $z_i$. Similarly, the notation $g(z_i,x_i^j,y_i^j)$ will mean the
state $z_{j+1}$.

An encoder is referred to as {\it information lossless} (IL) if for every
$z_i\in\calZ$, every positive integer $n$
and all
$(x_i^{i+n},y_i^{i+n})$, the quadruplet
$(z_i,f(z_i,x_i^{i+n},y_i^{i+n}),g(z_i,x_i^{i+n},y_i^{i+n}),x_i^{i+n})$ uniquely
determines $y_i^{i+n}$.
Given an encoder $E$ and a pair of input strings $(x^n,y^n)$, the compression ratio of
$y^n$ given $x^n$ w.r.t.\ $E$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
\rho_E(y^n|x^n)\dfn \frac{L(u^n)}{n}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\ell[f(z_i,x_i,y_i)]
\end{equation}
where $L(u^n)$ is the length (in bits) of the binary string $u^n$, which is
the sum of lengths $\ell(u_i)=\ell[f(z_i,x_i,y_i)]$.

The set of all IL encoders $\{E\}$ with no more than $s$
states will be denoted by $\calE(s)$.
The minimum of $\rho_E(y^n|x^n)$ over all encoders in $\calE(s)$
will be denoted by $\rho_s(y^n|x^n)$, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\rho_s(y^n|x^n)=\min_{E\in \calE(s)}\rho_E(y^n|x^n).
\end{equation}
Finally, let
\begin{equation}
\rho_s(\by|\bx)=\limsup_{n\to\infty}\rho_s(y^n|x^n),
\end{equation}
and define the {\it finite--state compressibility} of $\bx$ as
\begin{equation}
\rho(\by|\bx)=\lim_{s\to\infty}\rho_s(\by|\bx).
\end{equation}

In \cite{Ziv85}, the notion of the LZ complexity was extended to incorporate
finite-state lossless compression in the presence of side information, namely,
the conditional version of the LZ complexity.
Given $x^n$ and $y^n$,
let us apply the incremental
parsing procedure of the LZ algorithm
to the sequence of pairs $((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2),\ldots,(x_n,y_n))$.
As mentioned before, according to this procedure, all phrases are distinct
with a possible exception of the last phrase, which might be incomplete.
Let $c(x^n,y^n)$ denote the number of distinct phrases.
For example,\footnote{The same example appears in \cite{Ziv85}.} if
\begin{eqnarray}
x^6&=&0~|~1~|~0~1~|~0~1|\nonumber\\
y^6&=&0~|~1~|~0~0~|~0~1|\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
then $c(x^6,y^6)=4$.
Let $c(x^n)$ denote the resulting number of distinct phrases
of $n^n$, and let $x(l)$ denote the $l$-th distinct $x$--phrase,
$l=1,2,\ldots,c(x^n)$. In the above example, $c(x^6)=3$. Denote by
$c_l(y^n|x^n)$ the number of occurrences of $x(l)$ in the
parsing of $x^n$, or equivalently, the number of distinct $y$-phrases
that jointly appear with $x(l)$. Clearly, $\sum_{l=1}^{c(x^n)} c_l(y^n|x^n)=
c(x^n,y^n)$. In the above example, $x(1)=0$, $x(2)=1$, $x(3)=01$,
$c_1(y^6|x^6)=c_2(y^6|x^6)=1$, and $c_3(y^6|x^6)=2$. Now, the conditional LZ
complexity of $y^n$ given $x^n$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
\rho_{LZ}(y^n|x^n)\dfn\frac{1}{n}\sum_{l=1}^{c(x^n)}c_l(y^n|x^n)\log
c_l(y^n|x^n).
\end{equation}
In \cite{Ziv85} it was shown that $\rho_{LZ}(y^n|x^n)$ is the main term of the
compression ratio achieved by the conditional version of the LZ algorithm
described therein (see
also \cite{UK03}), i.e., the length function, $LZ(y^n|x^n)$, of the coding scheme
proposed therein is upper bounded (in parallel to (\ref{lz-clogc})) by
\begin{equation}
\label{conditional-lz}
LZ(y^n|x^n)\le n\rho_{LZ}(y^n|x^n)+n\epsilon_3(n),
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon_3(n)$ is a certain sequence that tends to zero as $n\to\infty$.
On the other hand, analogously to \cite[Theorem 1]{ZL78}, it was shown in
\cite{me00}, that $\rho_{LZ}(y^n|x^n)$ is also the main term of a lower bound
to the compression ratio that can be achieved by any finite-state encoder with
side information at both ends, provided that the number of states is not
too large, similarly as described above for the unconditional version. I.e.,
\begin{equation}
\rho_s(y^n|x^n)\ge \rho_{LZ}(y^n|x^n)-\epsilon_4(n,s).
\end{equation}

Another interesting property of IL finite-state encoders is the generalized
Kraft inequality \cite[Lemma 2]{ZL78}, which we state here slightly
differently than in \cite{ZL78}.\\

\noindent
{\bf Lemma 2.}
For every IL encoder with $s$ states and every initial state $z\in\calZ$,
\begin{equation}
K(z,x^\ell)=\sum_{y^\ell\in\calY^\ell}2^{-L[f(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)]}\le
s\cdot\left[1+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta^\ell}{s}\right)\right].
\end{equation}
The proof is exactly as the proof of Lemma 2 of \cite{ZL78} except that since
the initial state is fixed, the number $k_j$ of different $\{y^\ell\}$ with
$L[f(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)]=j$ cannot exceed $s\cdot 2^j$ (rather than $s^2 2^j$ in
\cite{ZL78}). Defining the NOB empirical joint distribution,
\begin{equation}
\hat{P}_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)=\frac{\ell}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/\ell-1}
\calI\{z_{i\ell+1}=z,~x_{i\ell+1}^{i\ell+\ell}=x^\ell,~y_{i\ell+1}^{i\ell+\ell}=y^\ell\},
\end{equation}
and accordingly also the marginal and the conditional distributions,
$\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}z|x^\ell)$
and $\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(y^\ell|x^\ell,z)$,
we observe that
\begin{eqnarray}
& &s\cdot\left[1+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta^\ell}{s}\right)\right]\nonumber\\
&\ge&\sum_{y^\ell\in\calY^\ell}2^{-L[f(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)]}\nonumber\\
&=&\sum_{y^\ell\in\calY^\ell}\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(y^\ell|x^\ell,z)\cdot
2^{-L[f(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)]-\log\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(y^\ell|x^\ell,z)}\nonumber\\
&=&\exp_2\left\{-\sum_{y^\ell\in\calY^\ell}\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(y^\ell|x^\ell,z)\cdot
L[f(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)]-\sum_{y^\ell}\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(y^\ell|x^\ell,z)\log\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(y^\ell|x^\ell,z)\right\}\nonumber\\
&=&\exp_2\left\{\hH(Y^\ell|X^\ell=x^\ell,Z=z)-\sum_{y^\ell\in\calY^\ell}\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(y^\ell|x^\ell,z)\cdot
L[f(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)]\right\},
\end{eqnarray}
which implies that
\begin{equation}
\sum_{y^\ell\in\calY^\ell}\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(y^\ell|x^\ell,z)\cdot L[f(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)]\ge
\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^\ell|X^\ell=x^\ell,Z=z)-\log\left\{s\cdot\left[1+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta^\ell}{s}\right)\right]\right\}.
\end{equation}
Averaging both sides w.r.t. $\hat{P}_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(x^\ell,z)$, we end up with
\begin{equation}
\sum_{(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)\in\calZ\times\calX^\ell\times\calY^\ell}\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)\cdot
L[f(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)]\ge
\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^\ell|X^\ell,Z)-\log\left\{s\cdot\left[1+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta^\ell}{s}\right)\right]\right\}.
\end{equation}
Now, similarly as eq.\ (33) of \cite{ZL78}
\begin{eqnarray}
\rho_E(x^n)&=&\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}L[f(z_i,x_i,y_i)]\nonumber\\
&=&\frac{\ell}{n\ell}\sum_{i=0}^{n/\ell-1}
L[f(z_{i\ell+1},x_{i\ell+1}^{i\ell+\ell},y_{i\ell+1}^{i\ell+\ell})]\nonumber\\
&=&\frac{1}{\ell}\sum_{(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)\in\calZ\times\calX^\ell\times\calY^\ell}\hat{P}_{\mbox{\tiny
nob}}(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)\cdot
L[f(z,x^\ell,y^\ell)]\nonumber\\
&\ge&\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^\ell|X^\ell,Z)}{\ell}-
\frac{1}{\ell}\log\left\{s\cdot\left[1+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta^\ell}{s}\right)\right]\right\}\nonumber\\
&=&\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^\ell|X^\ell)-\hat{I}_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Z;Y^\ell|X^\ell)}{\ell}-
\frac{1}{\ell}\log\left\{s\cdot\left[1+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta^\ell}{s}\right)\right]\right\}\nonumber\\
&\ge&\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^\ell|X^\ell)-\hat{H}_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Z)}{\ell}-
\frac{1}{\ell}\log\left\{s\cdot\left[1+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta^\ell}{s}\right)\right]\right\}\nonumber\\
&\ge&\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^\ell|X^\ell)-\log s}{\ell}-
\frac{1}{\ell}\log\left\{s\cdot\left[1+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta^\ell}{s}\right)\right]\right\}\nonumber\\
&\ge&\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^\ell|X^\ell)}{\ell}-
\frac{1}{\ell}\log\left\{s^2\cdot\left[1+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta^\ell}{s}\right)\right]\right\}.
\end{eqnarray}
and so, we have proved that
\begin{equation}
\rho_s(y^n|x^n)\ge\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^\ell|X^\ell)}{\ell}-\Delta_\ell(s,\beta).
\end{equation}
Consider now the application of the conditional LZ78 algorithm along blocks of length
$k$, where after each such block the LZ algorithm is restarted independently
of previous blocks. Since this is actually a block code of block length $k$
and a block code can be implemented using a finite-state encoder with
$s=(\alpha\beta)^k$ states, then assuming that $k$ divides $n$, we have:
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}LZ(y_{ik+1}^{ik+k}|x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})
&\ge&\rho_{(\alpha\beta)^k}(y^n|x^n)\nonumber\\
&\ge&\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^\ell|X^\ell)}{\ell}-\Delta_\ell(\alpha^k\beta^k,\beta).
\end{eqnarray}
On the other hand,
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}LZ(y_{ik+1}^{ik+k}|x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})
&=&\frac{k}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}\frac{LZ(y_{ik+1}^{ik+k}|x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})}{k}\nonumber\\
&\le&\frac{k}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}\rho_{\mbox{\tiny
LZ}}(y_{ik+1}^{ik+k}|x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})+\epsilon_3(k),
\end{eqnarray}
and so,
\begin{equation}
\frac{k}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}\rho_{\mbox{\tiny
LZ}}(y_{ik+1}^{ik+k}|x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})\ge
\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^\ell|X^\ell)}{\ell}-
\Delta_\ell(\alpha^{k}\beta^k,\beta)-\epsilon_3(k),
\end{equation}
where the interesting regime is according to $n\gg\ell\gg k\gg 1$.

Consider next a scenario of compressing $y^n$ given $x^n$ by a finite-state encoder with
$s$ states that
is allowed to vary once in every block of length $k$. The compression ratio,
which is $\frac{k}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}\rho_s(y_{ik+1}^{ik+k}|x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})$, is lower
bounded by
\begin{equation}
\frac{k}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}\rho_s(y_{ik+1}^{ik+k}|x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})\ge
\frac{k}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}\rho_{\mbox{\tiny
LZ}}(y_{ik+1}^{ik+k}|x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})-\epsilon_4(k,s).
\end{equation}
On the other hand, the best time-varying finite-state encoder with
$s=(\alpha\beta)^{m}$ states ($m$- positive integer)
cannot be worse than the best time-invariant code with the same number of
states. Let $m$ divide $n$ and consider block encoding of non-overlapping
blocks of length $m$ using a Shannon code with a conditional length function
\begin{equation}
L(y_{im+1}^{im+m}|x_{im+1}^{im+m})=\lceil -\log
Q(y_{im+1}^{im+m}|x_{im+1}^{im+m})\rceil,
~~~~i=0,1,2,\ldots,\frac{n}{m}-1
\end{equation}
Next, observe that
\begin{eqnarray}
L(y^n|x^n)&=&\sum_{i=0}^{n/m-1}L(y_{im+1}^{im+m}|x_{im+1}^{im+m})\nonumber\\
&\le&-\sum_{i=0}^{n/m-1}\log Q(y_{im+1}^{im+m}|x_{im+1}^{im+m})+\frac{n}{m}\nonumber\\
&=&-\frac{n}{m}\sum_{x^m,y^m}\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(x^m,y^m)\log Q(y^m|x^m)+\frac{n}{m},
\end{eqnarray}
Thus,
\begin{equation}
\frac{k}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}\rho_{\alpha^{2m}}(y_{ik+1}^{ik+k}|x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})\le
-\frac{1}{m}\sum_{x^m,y^m}\hP_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(x^m,y^m)\log Q(y^m|x^m)+\frac{1}{m},
\end{equation}
and since this holds for every $Q(\cdot|\cdot)$, it also holds for the
minimizing $Q(\cdot|\cdot)$, which yields
\begin{equation}
\frac{k}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}\rho_{\alpha^{2m}}(y_{ik+1}^{ik+k}|x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})\le
\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^m|X^m)}{m}
+\frac{1}{m}.
\end{equation}
Here the relevant regime is $k\gg m\gg 1$ and $n\gg m$. Of course, $n$ should
be a multiple of $k$,
but it does not have to be much larger than $k$. In fact, we may even have
$n=k$. However, combining it with the reverse inequalities, 
\begin{eqnarray}
& &\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny nob}}(Y^\ell|X^\ell)}{\ell}-
\Delta_\ell(\alpha^{k}\beta^k,\alpha)-\epsilon_1(k)\nonumber\\
&\le&\frac{k}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n/k-1}\rho_{\mbox{\tiny
LZ}}(y_{ik+1}^{ik+k}x_{ik+1}^{ik+k})\nonumber\\
&\le&\frac{\hH_{\mbox{\tiny
nob}}(Y^m|X^m)}{m}+\frac{1}{m}+\epsilon_2(k,\alpha^m\beta^m).
\end{eqnarray}
then we must have
$n\gg\ell\gg k\gg m\gg 1$.
