The proposed two-step approach is inspired by the model-free funnel control framework presented in \cite{PPCfeedback} and \cite{hard_soft}, where a similar backstepping-like methodology was employed for funnel-based control of pure feedback systems and Euler-Lagrange systems, respectively.

\subsection{Stage I}
Given a reference signal $x_{ref}(t)$, we define the tracking error as 
$$e_x(t) = x(t) - x_{ref}(t).$$ 
To regulate this error, we introduce exponentially decaying funnel constraints $\rho_x: \R_0^+ \rightarrow \R^n$, given by:
$$\rho_x(t) = e^{-\mu_x t}(p_x-q_x) + q_x,$$ 
where $p_x \in \R^n$ specifies the initial width of the funnel, satisfying $|e_x(0)| \preceq p_x$, $q_x \in \R^n$ determine the ultimate bound on the tracking error, with $\textbf{0}^{n \times 1} \prec q_x \prec p_x$, and $\mu_x \in \R^{n \times n}$ is a diagonal matrix with $\mu_x \succ \textbf{0}^{n \times n}$, dictates the decay rates of the funnel constrants.

We enforce tracking by constraining the tracking error $e_x(t)$ to remain within the funnel for all $t \in \R_0^+$:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:fun}
    -\rho_x(t) \prec e_x(t) \prec \rho_x(t). 
\end{equation}
To facilitate control design, we define the normalized error
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:norm_err}
    \varepsilon_x(t) = \text{diag}(\rho_{x})^{-1}e_x(t).    
\end{equation}
The velocity-level control input $v_r(t)$ is then formulated as 
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:velcon}
    v_r(t) = -\overline{v}\text{diag}(\Psi(\varepsilon_x)),
\end{equation}
where the map $\Psi:\R \rightarrow \R$ is described in Section \ref{sec:clamp}, and $\overline{v} \in \R^n$ is the maximum permissible velocity.

This velocity control law ensures that the tracking error remains bounded within the prescribed funnel constraints, thereby meeting the desired trajectory tracking specifications. 
