\subsection{Stage II}
To ensure smooth tracking of the reference velocity $v_{r}(t)$ from Stage I, as given in \eqref{eqn:velcon}, we now regulate the velocity error $e_v(t)$, defined as:
$$e_v(t) = v(t) - v_{r}(t).$$ 
To bound this error, we introduce exponentially decaying funnel constraints $\rho_v: \R_0^+ \rightarrow \R^n$, given by:
$$\rho_v(t) = e^{-\mu_v t}(p_v-q_v) + q_v,$$ 
where $p_v \in \R^n$ represents the initial width of the funnel, satisfying $|e_v(0)| \preceq p_v$, $q_v \in \R^n$ specifies the ultimate bound on the velocity error, with $0^{n \times 1} \prec q_v \prec p_v$, and $\mu_v > \textbf{0}^{n \times n} \in \R^{n \times n}$ is a diagonal matrix, controlling the decay rate of the funnel constraint.

The velocity error $e_v$ is constrained to remain within the funnel: 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:fun2}
    -\rho_v(t) \prec e_v(t) \prec \rho_v(t).    
\end{equation}
Next, we define the normalized velocity error $\varepsilon_v(t)$ as:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:norm_err2}
    \varepsilon_v(t) = \text{diag}(\rho_{v})^{-1}e_v(t).
\end{equation}
The final control input $\tau(t)$ at the acceleration level is then formulated as:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:con}
    \tau(t) = -\overline{\tau}\text{diag}(\Psi(\varepsilon_v)),
\end{equation}
where $\Psi:\R \rightarrow \R$ is the same bounding function described in Section \ref{sec:clamp}, and $\overline{\tau} \in \R^n$ is the maximum permissible torque.

As shown in \cite{hard_soft}, prescribed transient and steady-state performance specifications can be enforced by constraining the position- and velocity-level tracking errors within their respective time-varying funnels defined in \eqref{eqn:fun} and \eqref{eqn:fun2}. This is achieved by ensuring that the normalized errors in \eqref{eqn:norm_err} and \eqref{eqn:norm_err2} remain strictly inside the set $(-1,1)^n$. However, in contrast to \cite{hard_soft} the control law proposed in this work additionally adheres to prescribed input constraints, resulting in bounded control inputs and smooth trajectory tracking. To achieve this, we now introduce the bounded transformation functions.

\subsection{{Bounded Transformation Function}} \label{sec:clamp}
The bounded transformation function $\Psi:\R^n \rightarrow \R^n$ is a continuously differentiable mapping that ensures that the control remains bounded within the control limits while maintaining the desired behavior. In the paper, we introduce two categories of bounded transformation functions and discuss their properties.
\subsubsection{{Saturation Transformation Function}}
We define the saturation class of continuously differentiable functions as $\Psi(s) = [\Psi_1(s_1), \ldots, \Psi_n(s_n)]^\top$, where for all $i = [1;n]$:
$$\Psi_i(s_i) = 
\begin{cases} 
  -1, & s_i \in (-\infty,-1], \\
  0, & s_i = 0, \\
  1, & s_i \in [1,\infty),
\end{cases}
$$
and $\Psi_i(s_i)$ is nondecreasing for all $s_i \in (-\infty,\infty)$.
% $$\Psi'_i(s_i) \geq 0, \forall s_i \in (-\infty,\infty).
% \begin{cases} 
%   \geq 0, & x \in (-\infty,\infty), \\
%   = 0, & x = 0
% \end{cases}
% $$
Figure \ref{fig:sat} depicts a few examples of saturation function:
\begin{align*}
\Psi_1(s_1) = \tanh(as_1), \
\Psi_2(s_2) = \frac{e^{as_2}-1}{e^{as_2}+1}, \
\Psi_3(s_3) = \tanh(as_3)\left(1-e^{-(as_3)^2}\right) 
\end{align*}
with $a=5$, and as $a$ increases, we get sharper approximations of the saturation function.

