\subsection{Two-link SCARA manipulator}
The first case involves a tracking control problem for a 2R manipulator with two rotating joints. The joint angles, $\theta_1$ and $\theta_2$, define the configuration of the system as $x(t) = [\theta_1(t), \theta_2(t)]^\top$. The system dynamics is given by:
\begin{gather}\label{eqn:rr}
    ml^2
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \frac{5}{3} + c_2 & \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{2}c_2 \\
        \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{2}c_2 & \frac{1}{3}
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \Ddot{\theta}_1 \\
        \Ddot{\theta}_2
    \end{bmatrix} 
    +
    ml^2s_2
    \begin{bmatrix}
        -\frac{1}{2}\dot{\theta}_2^2 - \dot{\theta}_1\dot{\theta}_2\\
        \frac{1}{2}\dot{\theta}_1^2
    \end{bmatrix}
    + mgl
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \frac{3}{2}c_1 + \frac{1}{2}c_{12} \\
        \frac{1}{2}c_{12}
    \end{bmatrix}
    = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \tau_1(t) \\
        \tau_2(t)
    \end{bmatrix}
    + d(t), \nonumber
\end{gather}
% where $m = 3 kg$ and $l = 1 m$ are the mass and length of each link, $g = 9.8m/s^2$ is the acceleration due to gravity, 
where are the mass $m$ and length $l$ of each link, the acceleration due to gravity $g$, and the bounded disturbance $d(t)$ are all \textbf{unknown}, while $\tau_1(t), \tau_2(t)$ represent the joint torque inputs. Here, $c_1 = \cos{\theta_1}$, $c_2 = \cos{\theta_2}$, $s_2 = \sin{\theta_2}$, and $c_{12} = \cos{(\theta_1 + \theta_2)}$. 
The system is constrained by the maximum joint velocity $\overline{v} = 6$ rad/s and the maximum torque $\overline{\tau} = 10$ N-m, respectively. 
We design the velocity-level funnel with parameters $p_x = 0.2, q_x = 0.02, \mu_x = 0.1$, and the acceleration-level funnel with $p_v = 2, q_v = 0.02, \mu_v = 0.1$.

We define the bounds from Assumptions \ref{assum_tau} and \ref{assum_Md} as $\underline{m}=1.5 \text{ kg}, \underline{m}_i = 1.6 \text{ kg}^{-1}$, and the bound from Assumption \ref{assum_V} as $\max{(-\underline{V}_M, \overline{V}_M)} = 5 m^2/s^2$, adhering to the system dynamics in Equation \eqref{eqn:rr}. Using the feasibility conditions in Equations \eqref{eqn:feas1} and \eqref{eqn:feas2}, we determine the maximum permissible disturbance in Assumption \ref{assum_d} as $\overline{d} = 2 N-m$. 

{Provided the disturbance remains within bounds established from the feasibility conditions in Equations \eqref{eqn:feas1} and \eqref{eqn:feas2}, the proposed control law in Equation \eqref{eqn:con} effectively keeps state errors within the prescribed funnel boundaries, ensuring safe tracking, as demonstrated in Figure \ref{fig:sim_rr}.}

\subsection{7-DOF Franka Research 3 Manipulator}
The robotic manipulator used in the second case study is a Franka Research 3 (Figure \ref{fig:franka}), a lightweight 7-DOF robot designed for human-robot collaborative applications \cite{FRANKA}. For this study, the maximum allowable joint velocity and joint torque are set to $\overline{v} = 6$ rad/s and $\overline{\tau} = 8$ N-m, respectively \cite{FRANKA2, FRANKA3}. The velocity and acceleration levels are designed for all joints with the parameters $p_x = 1, q_x = 0.1, \mu_x = 0.5$, and $p_v = 2, q_v = 0.1, \mu_v = 0.5$, respectively.

This case study demonstrates the scalability of the proposed approach. We conducted three experiments: (i) nominal task execution, (ii) dynamics alteration by attaching a water bottle to illustrate robustness to \textbf{unknown system parameters}, and (iii) applying sudden jerks to demonstrate \textbf{disturbance rejection}. In all cases, the controller achieved effective tracking while respecting \textbf{input constraints}, validating its suitability for complex, high-dimensional systems. Hardware results are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:simFRANKA1}, with videos available \href{https://drive.google.com/file/d/15S_USJ11sCcmMG9JdmNWkoCA4rXxKiZf/view?usp=sharing}{here}.

